window.CYCLE3_PAGE = {"html": "<nav class=\"crumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><a href=\"#home\">Overview</a><span>/</span><span>Cycle 3</span></nav><header class=\"guide-heading\"><p class=\"kicker\">Cycle 3 | Guided explanation</p><h1>Cycle 3: Support teacher growth through coaching</h1><p>Cycle 3 asks you to work with a volunteer teacher to understand a lesson, agree on a focused observation, examine evidence together, and decide what professional learning should follow. You are demonstrating a coaching partnership, not giving the teacher a performance rating.</p><p>The teacher's voice, the selected CSTP elements, observation evidence, lesson recording, and student work need to remain connected. We will follow all ten parts and keep the different recordings and commentaries distinct.</p></header><nav class=\"c1-contents\" aria-label=\"Cycle 3 sections\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/limits\">Submission limits</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/foundation\">Understanding the work</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-map\">Rubric map</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/fictional-case\">Fictional example</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">Stronger explanations</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-a\">Part A</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-b\">Part B</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-c\">Part C</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-d\">Part D</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-e\">Part E</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-f\">Part F</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-g\">Part G</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-h\">Part H</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-i\">Part I</a><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-j\">Part J</a></nav><article class=\"reading guide-reading c1-reading\"><section id=\"limits\"><h2>What you submit, and the limits to plan around</h2><p>Read this before arranging meetings or collecting evidence. These are maxima, not targets. The original narrative template counts toward its page limit. N/A in the guide means no page or time maximum is specified for that part; it does not remove the evidence requirement.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Limit and files</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Classroom context, lesson, and observation</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Two pre-observation meeting clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Exactly 2 video files; up to 6 minutes each</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">E</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Commentary on the pre-observation meeting</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">F</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">G</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work used in the post-observation meeting</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">At least 1 example; 1 file; document or video; no maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">H</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Post-observation meeting clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1-3 clips; each at least 1 minute; up to 15 minutes TOTAL</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">I</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Commentary on the post-observation meeting</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 written pages OR 6 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Observe the lesson live (synchronously, while it is taught) for at least 20 minutes and record the full observation. Share the lesson recording with the teacher before the post-observation meeting, and jointly view selected lesson evidence during that meeting. The lesson recording itself is not the submitted pre- or post-meeting footage.</p><p>Part D is exactly two clips of up to six minutes each. Part H is 1-3 clips with at least one minute per clip and at most 15 minutes total. Parts E and I each allow up to eight written pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL; Part J allows four pages OR six minutes. Do not interchange these limits.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Original submission specifications</a></p></section><section id=\"foundation\"><h2>Understand the work before filling in the templates</h2><h3>Begin with partnership</h3><p>The teacher brings knowledge of students, content, prior instruction, and professional learning needs. You contribute a process that helps both of you examine practice. Facilitative questions invite the teacher to interpret evidence and consider possibilities; they are not leading questions whose only acceptable answer is your preferred solution.</p><h3>Choose a focus narrow enough to observe</h3><p>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, using the teacher's self-assessment, lesson, and students' assets and needs. Agree on what evidence would be relevant. A broad interest in engagement needs to become an observable question before it can guide data collection. Use the applicable CSTP version and an explicit crosswalk when the site uses another framework.</p><h3>Keep the evidence roles distinct</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it establishes</th><th scope=\"col\">What it does not establish alone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Lesson plan</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Intended learning, instruction, supports, and work product.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">What actually happened.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and lesson video</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Events and interactions during the observed lesson.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">The meaning of every student's experience or lasting learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">What particular learners demonstrated on the task.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">Why the result occurred, without further analysis.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Meeting clips and commentary</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">The coaching interaction and your analysis of it.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">An event not present in the submitted evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section id=\"rubric-map\"><h2>How the parts and rubrics fit together</h2><p>Each row names one rubric. Read its purpose now, then return to the complete five-level rubric beside the relevant work. A higher level includes the stated earlier levels; extra pages, polished language, or more charts alone do not establish it.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-overview-map\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Rubric focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">1. Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Understand the school and the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\"><strong>3.1: Coaching context</strong></a><br>Analyze current practices and tailor the coaching cycle to this teacher.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">2. Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B-E</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Listen, select the focus together, and agree on evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\"><strong>3.2: Planning a coaching partnership</strong></a><br>Analyze a two-way pre-meeting grounded in students, lesson goals, and selected CSTP elements.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">F-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Recognize and analyze teaching in relation to learning.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\"><strong>3.3: Focused observation evidence</strong></a><br>Document useful, specific evidence linked to the selected elements and content goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">F-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Co-analyze evidence, strengths, and growth.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\"><strong>3.4: A learning conversation</strong></a><br>Use notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive partnership.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">F-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Turn findings into supported professional learning.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\"><strong>3.5: Shared growth steps</strong></a><br>Co-determine evidence-based next steps, resources, and coaching support.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">4. Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Use teacher feedback to examine your coaching.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\"><strong>3.6: Reflection on coaching</strong></a><br>Analyze strengths, growth, and evidence of maintaining a coaching partnership.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">4. Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Consider the potential of coaching within continuous improvement.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\"><strong>3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</strong></a><br>Connect equitable leadership, your coaching role, and potential teacher growth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section id=\"fictional-case\"><h2>Fictional Example Case</h2><p class=\"source-note\">All school context, people, meetings, work samples, quotations, counts, and outcomes in this Canyon View case are invented for instruction. This is not Jordan High data, an assessed submission, or evidence that fieldwork occurred. Illustrative video moments are not recordings or scripts to stage.</p><p>The volunteer teacher is Chen, one of the fictional Canyon View colleagues introduced in Cycle 2. Her participation here is a separate, voluntary coaching partnership, not an automatic continuation imposed by the earlier group. She chooses to examine how assessment during a Grade 9 writing lesson informs her response to students.</p><p>They jointly select two assessment-domain elements in the 2024 CSTP, 5A and 5B. A 28-minute lesson produces initial and revised paragraphs. The post-meeting compares timestamped observation notes, jointly viewed lesson segments, and three contrasting work examples. Chen also gives feedback about the candidate's coaching. The inquiry concerns this class of 28; it is distinct from Cycle 2's 24-student sample.</p><p>The case shows useful reasoning and limitations, not a model guaranteed to earn a score. A real teacher may choose a different focus, and that choice should follow the evidence and partnership rather than a desire to copy the example.</p></section><section id=\"stronger-writing\" class=\"writing-intro\"><h2>Write beyond description</h2><p>Strong explanations make your reasoning visible: what the evidence shows, why you acted, how others shaped the work, and what remains uncertain. More confident language or a longer response does not create stronger evidence.</p><p>These comparisons draw on a critical review of three historical Version 8 examples. <strong>Both paragraphs in each comparison are newly written fictional teaching examples, not quotations or rewrites of a candidate submission.</strong> They illustrate selected writing decisions, not complete answers or assigned rubric levels. The practice records are separate from Cedar Grove, Canyon View, Harbor High, and public school data.</p><ol class=\"writing-index\"><li><a href=\"#cycle/c3/writing-c3-context\">Part A: Separate coaching conditions from coaching impact</a></li><li><a href=\"#cycle/c3/writing-c3-focus\">Part E: Connect a shared focus to observable evidence</a></li><li><a href=\"#cycle/c3/writing-c3-observation\">Part F: Distinguish what you observed from what it means</a></li><li><a href=\"#cycle/c3/writing-c3-analysis\">Part I: Let student work complicate a positive impression</a></li><li><a href=\"#cycle/c3/writing-c3-feedback\">Part J: Learn from the support you did not yet provide</a></li></ol><p>Read the evidence record first and identify what the developing explanation overstates or leaves unexplained. Then compare the stronger explanation. Apply the questions to your own authentic work; do not copy the paragraphs, invent missing evidence, or stage a meeting to resemble an example. Where evidence is missing, acknowledge the limit and plan the needed work.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Current Version 09 prompts, limits, and full rubrics govern. These contrasts do not establish that the historical samples passed or that a longer response would earn a higher score. <a href=\"https://www.ctcexams.nesinc.com/TestView.aspx?f=HTML_FRAG/CalAPA_AssessmentMaterials.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Current assessment materials</a> &middot; <a href=\"https://www.ctcexams.nesinc.com/TestView.aspx?f=CACBT_Faculty_CalAPA.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Acceptable candidate support</a>.</p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"A\"><h2 id=\"part-a\">Part A: School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">1. Investigate | Up to 5 pages; 1 file</p><p>Start with the actual school and teacher, not a coaching script. Learn what observation and feedback currently mean in this setting, how teachers influence the process, and what the volunteer teacher has experienced. Those details affect trust and what support will be useful.</p><p>A volunteer partnership does not make you the teacher's evaluator. Clarify your role, the assessment purpose, permissions, and how evidence will be handled. The teacher's priorities and students' learning needs should shape the inquiry.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\" download>Download the original Part A template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>1. Outline how often during the year teachers are coached, observed, or provided instructional feedback and what the intended outcomes are for the school, teachers, and students. After reviewing current practices at this school, provide an analysis of the effectiveness of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Separate coaching intended for growth from evaluative observations. Explain frequency, purpose, and the evidence you have for effectiveness; do not assume either process is effective because a calendar exists.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Canyon View schedules two administrator observations each year and offers voluntary coaching during a monthly inquiry period. The evaluation cycle checks performance expectations; the voluntary cycle is intended to help teachers examine a chosen instructional need. Teachers report that timely work-focused feedback is useful, but available records seldom connect follow-up to student work. The schedule establishes access to feedback, while its effect on teaching and learning remains incompletely documented.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Describe the practices at your actual site, even if they are limited or different. Analyze what the available evidence supports and what it cannot establish.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>2. Describe the processes, forms, and/or documents that are used to guide pre-observation meetings, collect observation data, and focus post-observation meetings (e.g., teacher response forms, observation tools, student work products, next steps for professional growth). If the school does not conduct teacher observations and uses a different approach to provide instructional feedback to teachers, provide a detailed description of the process(es) and forms used.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain the tools and processes actually used before, during, and after observation. If the site uses a different feedback process, describe it accurately.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The school uses a pre-conference form with a teacher goal, an observation note sheet with timestamps, and a post-conference form with strengths and next steps. The forms can support a connected inquiry, but recent examples emphasize teacher actions more than student responses. I will discuss with Chen how our notes can include both the instructional opportunity and evidence of student understanding.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A form's existence does not show how it is used. Explain its affordances and limitations for this particular coaching partnership.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>3. Explain the level of input teachers have on the focus or purpose of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe how much influence teachers have over the focus and purpose. Explain the implications for voice, trust, and ownership.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Teachers may request the voluntary coaching focus, while administrators set the evaluative observation schedule. Chen chose to examine assessment during writing instruction and asked that our work remain separate from evaluation. I clarified my non-evaluative role and the agreed handling of recordings before we set the focus.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Do not promise confidentiality beyond actual policy or your authority. Explain the real boundaries and how the teacher participates within them.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>4. Explain the implications for this cycle based on the analysis of the current role of coaching practices (at the school) and the background and experience of the volunteer teacher.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Use both the school context and this teacher's experience to explain how you will approach the cycle.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen has experience with instructional models but reports that earlier feedback arrived after the unit ended. We will schedule the post-observation conversation two days after the lesson and share the lesson recording beforehand. Because she wants to recognize student understanding during instruction, our pre-meeting will identify observable evidence rather than a broad goal such as engagement.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is the bridge from investigation to a tailored plan. The same generic coaching sequence for every teacher would not show how the investigation mattered.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>5. How do the CSTPs guide or play a role in teacher growth and development at the school? (If the school uses another set of standards, provide a crosswalk to the CSTPs for the element[s] you have identified.)</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how the CSTP guide professional growth at the site. If another framework is used, provide a crosswalk for the selected elements instead of simply declaring the frameworks equivalent.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Canyon View uses the 2024 CSTP in voluntary growth plans. We will consider elements 5A, Understanding and Using Assessments, and 5B, Interpreting and Using Assessment Data to Inform Student Learning, in the same assessment domain. We will connect the selected focus to lesson evidence and Chen's self-assessment rather than treat the standards as a checklist of every possible teaching behavior.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The selected version and element wording must be consistent across the plan, observation, and commentary. Consult <a href=\"https://docs.ctc.ca.gov/Document/Download/30328\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">2024 California Standards for the Teaching Profession</a>. A crosswalk is needed when your site uses a different framework.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Volunteer Teacher and Context for the Coaching Cycle</p><h3>1. Provide information about the volunteer teacher\u2019s:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. professional experience as a teacher (including years of service, school site[s], grades and content taught, languages)</p><p>b. current assignment (grade level[s], content area, course titles)</p><p>c. past experiences with coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p><p>d. knowledge and use of the CSTP to guide their growth and development as a teacher at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address all four subparts with relevant, appropriately obtained background: experience and languages, assignment, prior feedback, and CSTP knowledge.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen has taught for seven years at two secondary schools, previously Grades 7-8 English and now Grade 9 English. She speaks English and Mandarin and teaches three Grade 9 sections. She has participated in annual administrator observations and a voluntary peer lesson study. She found concrete work comparisons helpful but found generalized ratings difficult to act on. She has used CSTP elements in a growth plan but wants help making the assessment focus observable.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Language and cultural knowledge are assets, not automatic explanations of teaching effectiveness. Include the background that changes your coaching choices and protect identities.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Volunteer Teacher and Context for the Coaching Cycle</p><h3>2. Explain how the information you gathered about the school context and the volunteer teacher\u2019s experiences informs your plans for conducting a coaching cycle, including how you will:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. orient the volunteer teacher to participate in your coaching process</p><p>b. participate in a pre-observation meeting</p><p>c. prepare for the observation and collection of evidence (including student work)</p><p>d. jointly conduct (with the volunteer teacher) the post-observation meeting</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain each stage of the plan, not merely the dates. Show how the teacher will influence decisions and how student work enters the process.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Orientation will establish purpose, consent, roles, storage, and the teacher's right to ask questions. In the pre-meeting, Chen will assess her practice and review the lesson and students before we jointly select one or two elements. We will agree on timestamped notes and initial/revised paragraphs as evidence. I will record the full 28-minute observation, share the video with her before the post-meeting, and co-conduct a discussion using her interpretation, jointly viewed segments, and student work.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The four stages form one inquiry. Teacher ownership is visible in choices, not just in the fact that the teacher attended.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q8\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Volunteer Teacher and Context for the Coaching Cycle</p><h3>3. Describe any challenges you may face in conducting a coaching cycle with the volunteer teacher and provide strategies for addressing these challenges.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Anticipate actual partnership, scheduling, access, or evidence challenges and practical responses.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>A short planning period may encourage rushed conclusions, so we will reserve uninterrupted time and identify the evidence to review in advance. Chen worries the camera could make the lesson feel evaluative; we will clarify the non-evaluative purpose and permissions without promising anonymity that the recording cannot provide. We will test sound and framing and use authorized storage. If required consent is unavailable, I will work with the program and site on an appropriate alternative before recording.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A challenge should lead to a feasible response within policy. Do not solve a permission problem by recording anyway or fabricating evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-1\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: Coaching context</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school with the volunteer teacher, and explain the implications of how current practices impact their approach to conducting a coaching cycle?</p><p>Analyze current practices and tailor the coaching cycle to this teacher.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not provide an explanation of the purpose or processes for coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school. Candidate outlines a plan for the coaching cycle with little or no details OR does not outline a plan.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing purpose or a plan leaves the coaching approach unexplained.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate lists the teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school, providing limited details about the processes used. The purpose for conducting these processes is unclear. Candidate outlines a vague plan for the coaching cycle with minimal details about implications of each step of the coaching cycle and/or observation based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A list of practices and a vague plan do not show meaningful implications.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly describes and analyzes the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback, discussing the purpose and intended goals of the practices for both teachers and students. Candidate clearly describes a plan for and the implications of each step of the coaching cycle and observation based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze the setting and explain each stage using the teacher's experience.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes how teachers participate in the selection and purpose of the instructional feedback practices at the school to meet the unique needs of the teachers and how the practices build trust between the teachers and school administrators.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain teacher influence and how feedback practices build trust.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate discusses relevant evidence-based practices and/or adult learning theory to explain how school practices of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback support ongoing teacher development and benefit students\u2019 learning and well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use relevant evidence-based practice or adult-learning theory to explain benefits.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Coaching, Observation, and/or Instructional Feedback Practices at the School and the Volunteer Teacher (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 14 (printed page 10)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The plan responds to Chen's experience with delayed feedback and her request for a non-evaluative focus.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-context\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-context\">Separate coaching conditions from coaching impact</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Fictional school records show annual evaluation conferences, while voluntary coaching started this term. Schoolwide scores declined before the new coaching began; no evaluation of that coaching is available.</li><li>The volunteer teacher says prior feedback felt evaluative and requests help interpreting student explanations. The candidate is not the teacher&#x27;s evaluator.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>Declining school scores prove coaching is ineffective. I will use a standard coaching cycle with this teacher.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The schoolwide trend raises a concern but cannot establish the effect of a coaching process that began later. The teacher&#x27;s prior experience gives me a more immediate planning consideration: feedback has felt evaluative. I will clarify my non-evaluative role, invite the teacher to identify a focus, and agree on what evidence we will examine. The request to interpret student explanations should shape that conversation; it does not authorize me to select the teacher&#x27;s goal in advance.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Respects timing and the limits of schoolwide outcome data.</li><li>Uses the teacher&#x27;s experience to shape the partnership instead of merely describing their background.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>Which feature of this teacher&#x27;s experience changes your plan? Explain the implications without treating whole-school achievement trends as a direct measure of one coaching practice.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Rubric 3.1</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Have you analyzed school coaching practices and the volunteer teacher's experience, self-assessment, and learning needs?</li><li>Does the coaching plan respond to this teacher and explain your relationship, including relevant authority and trust?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-b\">Continue to Part B <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"B\"><h2 id=\"part-b\">Part B: Classroom context, lesson, and observation</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Up to 4 pages; 1 file</p><p>This part helps the reader understand the classroom in which the lesson and coaching focus make sense. The goal is not to list every fact you know about the class. Explain the content, students, setting, supports, and practical conditions that matter for interpreting instruction.</p><p>Gather this information with the teacher. A statement such as diverse learners is too broad to guide observation; identify relevant assets, interests, language resources, prior learning, and support needs without disclosing protected information.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_B_ClassroomContextLessonObservation.docx\" download>Download the original Part B template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>1. Classroom context, including:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. topic of lesson, learning and instructional goals, students\u2019 prior knowledge, and what was taught in the prior lesson</p><p>b. classroom setting (face-to-face, online, or hybrid) and organization for learning (whole class, small groups, individual practice, hybrid)</p><p>c. description of students served, including their assets and learning needs (e.g., grade level, demographics, student assets [cultural and/or linguistic] and interests, learning supports and adaptations)</p><p>d. materials and/or resources used during the lesson by the teacher or students to enhance or extend learning</p><p>e. other relevant classroom context information (e.g., time of day for the lesson, time of year, support staff that are in the room during the lesson, materials used, safety issues, bell schedule, parent support)</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address every context subpart. Describe the content goal, prior learning, setting and grouping, student assets and needs, materials, and relevant logistics. Include details that will affect what the observer can reasonably interpret.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The 28-minute face-to-face Grade 9 English lesson asks students to explain how textual evidence supports an argument. In the prior lesson, students selected quotations; nine of this class's 28 preliminary paragraphs explained their relevance. Students bring experience discussing fairness in stories and varied languages and community perspectives. Twelve are classified as English learners; supports are chosen from individual needs, not assigned uniformly by label. Three students have documented accommodations, which remain in place for this lesson. Students compare models as a class, rehearse explanations with a partner, and revise individually. Materials include the text excerpt, contrasting models, an explanation criterion, accessible directions, and optional language supports. Morgan co-teaches this midmorning lesson; a shortened bell schedule is not in effect.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This class of 28 is not the 24-student cross-class sample in the Cycle 2 case. Do not merge their denominators. In an actual response, explain specific relevant assets, adaptations, and logistics without exposing protected student information.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the four-page account establish the actual class, content goal, prior learning, learner assets, and needed access supports?</li><li>Are the jointly chosen CSTP elements, evidence plan, and lesson context consistent with the pre-meeting?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-c\">Continue to Part C <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"C\"><h2 id=\"part-c\">Part C: Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Use the volunteer teacher's lesson plan, including a description of the student work product. It should make the learning goal, planned instruction, and evidence of learning understandable. The candidate is not asked to replace the teacher's planning with a generic coach-authored lesson.</p><p>Review how students will show the intended learning and how the work will be available for the post-observation conversation. A plan describes intention; the lesson recording, observation notes, and actual work later show what occurred.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-C.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>Fictional lesson-plan excerpt</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Time</th><th scope=\"col\">Planned teaching and learning</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence and access</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">0-5 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Recall the claim and selected quotation; review the explanation criterion.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Initial short paragraph; accessible text and directions.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">5-10 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Compare a reasoned explanation with a repetition of evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Students identify why one explanation supports the claim.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">10-18 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Partner rehearsal, then an individual explanation.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Teacher checks responses; optional language supports and documented accommodations remain available.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">18-25 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Revise the paragraph after a check for reasoning.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Keep initial and revised versions; teacher responds to observed needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">25-28 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Explain which revision strengthened the argument and why.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Brief exit response and revised paragraph reveal understanding and remaining questions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The work-product description identifies the initial paragraph, revision, and explanation of the revision. The observation will examine how Chen gathers evidence and responds, not whether every planned minute is followed exactly. This excerpt is a fictional teaching aid, not an original CTC lesson-plan template.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Is this the volunteer teacher's lesson plan with a clear learning goal and description of the work students will produce?</li><li>Can the teacher and coach identify what evidence will be available for the later conversation?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-d\">Continue to Part D <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"D\"><h2 id=\"part-d\">Part D: Two pre-observation meeting clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Exactly 2 video files; up to 6 minutes each</p><p>Record the pre-observation meeting with the candidate and teacher seen and heard, following required permissions. Select two clips that together show the required planning work. These are clips of the adult meeting, not excerpts from the classroom lesson.</p><p>The conversation should include teacher self-assessment, review of the lesson and students, shared selection of one or two elements from the same CSTP domain, and agreement on the observation and evidence. Keep the teacher's reasoning visible, including how it influences decisions.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-D.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative meeting clip</th><th scope=\"col\">Length</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Illustrative meeting clip\">Pre-meeting Clip 1</td><td data-label=\"Length\">5:10</td><td data-label=\"Evidence coverage\">Teacher self-assessment; lesson plan, work product, context, and learner assets and needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Illustrative meeting clip\">Pre-meeting Clip 2</td><td data-label=\"Length\">5:00</td><td data-label=\"Evidence coverage\">Joint element selection; agreed observation process and specific evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Both clips fit the individual six-minute maximum. A combined duration below twelve minutes does not allow one seven-minute clip. These are invented selection illustrations; no staged or simulated interaction should be submitted as actual fieldwork.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are there exactly two authentic pre-observation meeting clips, each at most six minutes, with both adults seen and heard?</li><li>Do they show teacher self-assessment, review of students and lesson, shared CSTP selection, and agreement on evidence?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-e\">Continue to Part E <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"E\"><h2 id=\"part-e\">Part E: Commentary on the pre-observation meeting</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Address each prompt at least once across the two submitted clips. Identify the clip and a precise timestamp, then explain what you did, why, and the impact supported by the conversation. The commentary template's repeated boxes give you room for additional moments; they are not a request to invent additional requirements.</p><p>Analysis is more than a summary. \u201cI asked about prior learning\u201d describes an action. Explaining why that question was needed and how the teacher's response changed the evidence plan makes the leadership reasoning visible.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Download the original Part E template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-E-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-E-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>1. Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Self-Assessment: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher determined their instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element(s).</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how the teacher's self-assessment informed the shared understanding of instructional needs. Describe your listening and questioning, not only the need you selected.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 1, 00:35-01:45: Chen says students can identify evidence but she is not always sure who understands its connection to a claim. I ask what she notices in their work and which responses are hardest to interpret. She contrasts a correct quotation with a paragraph that explains why it matters. This makes the assessment need more specific before we choose the observation focus.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The analysis links a facilitative question to a teacher response. Your actual clip must show the evidence for the impact you claim.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>2. Lesson Plan Review: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher reviewed the lesson plan. Think about:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. student work product,</p><p>b. classroom context, and</p><p>c. students\u2019 assets and learning needs.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Connect review of the lesson plan to the work students will produce, the classroom setting, and learner assets and needs.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 1, 02:00-04:20: we review the initial/revised paragraph task and Chen describes students who explain ideas more fully in discussion than in writing. I ask how the task will make their thinking visible without lowering the reasoning goal. She proposes oral rehearsal and accessible models, followed by an individual written explanation. We agree to retain both versions of selected work.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Do not treat student needs as a list of labels. Explain how the discussion changes the task, support, or evidence plan.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>3. CSTP Selection: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher selected one or two CSTP elements based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s self-assessment and students\u2019 assets and learning needs.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show a joint selection of one or two elements in the same domain, grounded in self-assessment and students, not imposed by the candidate.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 2, 00:20-01:50: Chen identifies assessment as the focus. We compare 5A and 5B with her question about recognizing reasoning and responding while the lesson is underway. She proposes collecting both brief checks for understanding and the instructional responses that follow; together we retain these two elements in the assessment domain.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The teacher's contribution must influence the choice. The fact that you read element titles aloud does not establish joint selection.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>4. Coaching and Observation Process: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher collaboratively reviewed the coaching and observation process, and the CSTP\u2013element-focused data to be collected during the lesson.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how you agreed on the observation process and specific evidence, including student work.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 2, 02:10-04:35: I propose timestamped notes separating what is seen or heard from interpretation. Chen asks that we record which student responses prompt a change in instruction and retain initial/revised paragraphs from contrasting learners. We agree to record the full 28-minute lesson, share the recording with Chen in advance, and hold a post-observation meeting in which we view the video and analyze student work together.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A data plan should allow both adults to recognize what will be collected and why. A general promise to observe engagement would not answer the agreed assessment question.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-2\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Planning a coaching partnership</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher to understand the learning goals, classroom context, and student assets (e.g., cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests and learning needs; jointly select with the volunteer teacher one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, including evidence to be collected; and plan for the observation?</p><p>Analyze a two-way pre-meeting grounded in students, lesson goals, and selected CSTP elements.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate and the volunteer teacher do not discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs. Selection of the CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected, including student work, is made by the candidate without input from the volunteer teacher or element(s) are not selected. OR No plan is established for the coaching and/or observation process. OR In the commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">One-sided selection, missing planning, or absent analysis can leave essential evidence missing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate and volunteer teacher broadly discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs, including student work. Candidate minimally involves the volunteer teacher in the selection of CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected during the observation. It is not clear what aspects of instruction need to be the focus of the observation, and a plan is not specified and/or agreed to by the volunteer teacher OR candidate creates plan for observation without input from the volunteer teacher. In the commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis* of their ability to listen to and/or talk with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Minimal teacher involvement is not a clearly shared plan.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly engages the volunteer teacher in a detailed and focused pre-observation discussion about the classroom context, student assets and learning needs, and content-specific learning goals of the lesson. Candidate and the volunteer teacher jointly select the CSTP element(s) and together determine evidence to be collected during the observation, including student work. Candidate and volunteer teacher create a plan for the observation, clearly identifying the focal aspects of instruction. In the commentary, candidate provides an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Demonstrate focused discussion, joint selection, a data plan, and analysis of your communication.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies to engage in a two-way discussion with the volunteer teacher to draw out their specific areas of need in relation to learning goals, classroom context, student assets, CSTP element(s), and observation plans.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use facilitative questions to draw out the teacher's specific needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies, including re-directing as appropriate for the conversation, to deepen discussion regarding equitable opportunities for all students in the class to learn.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Deepen discussion of equitable learning opportunities, redirecting when appropriate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Note: *Analysis: What the candidate does, why they do it, and the impact their action had Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Classroom Context, Lesson, and Observation (up to 4 pages) \u2022 Part C: Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Lesson Plan, Including Student Work Product Description \u2022 Part D: 2 Video Clips of the Pre-Observation Meeting (up to 6 minutes each) \u2022 Part E: Commentary of the Pre-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 19 (printed page 15)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The fictional moments show how teacher self-assessment changes the focus and evidence plan; real submitted clips must demonstrate the candidate's own actions.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-focus\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-focus\">Connect a shared focus to observable evidence</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Invented pre-observation record: the teacher wants to distinguish students stating a science claim from explaining it with evidence. The teacher and candidate review the applicable CSTP wording and select an assessment-focused element together.</li><li>They agree to collect question-and-response notes during discussion and each learner&#x27;s independent claim-and-evidence exit response. The teacher requests that observations distinguish volunteered answers from the whole class&#x27;s understanding.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>We selected a CSTP and I will observe engagement. The teacher agreed with my plan.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher&#x27;s concern was that a few volunteered answers might overstate class understanding. We jointly connected that concern to the assessment-focused CSTP element in our plan. We agreed on two evidence sources: notes on the questions and responses during discussion, and independent claim-and-evidence exit responses. The teacher&#x27;s request changed the collection plan: I would not treat volunteered answers alone as evidence of everyone&#x27;s understanding.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Shows teacher agency through a request that actually shapes the plan.</li><li>Connects the focus to evidence that can answer the agreed question; does not equate agreement with a co-designed plan.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>In your full response, identify the exact agreed CSTP element and applicable wording, the teacher&#x27;s self-assessment, and the evidence plan. Check your current guide and standards; do not transfer an older sample&#x27;s element number without verifying it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Rubric 3.2</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are all four prompts addressed across the two clips with precise timestamps and analysis of your facilitation?</li><li>Does commentary agree with the actual planning conversation and fit eight pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-f\">Continue to Part F <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"F\"><h2 id=\"part-f\">Part F: Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Observe the lesson live (synchronously, while it is taught) for at least 20 minutes and record the full observation. This case uses a 28-minute lesson. Collect the specific evidence agreed on in the pre-meeting, including student work. Keep notes descriptive enough that the teacher can examine the event rather than accept your judgment on trust.</p><p>Separate observation from interpretation: what was said or done, when it happened, which selected element it informs, and what question it raises. Avoid unsupported labels such as disengaged or ineffective. A note can document a missed opportunity without pretending to know the teacher's intention.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-F.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>From an event to a useful coaching question</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Lesson time and observed evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Connection and cautious interpretation</th><th scope=\"col\">Question to examine together</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Lesson time and observed evidence\">08:20: Chen displays two explanations and asks which better supports the claim. Four students refer to the because clause; two responses only identify the quotation.</td><td data-label=\"Connection and cautious interpretation\">5A/5B: the comparison elicits evidence of understanding. Responses suggest different interpretations of the criterion, not a single whole-class conclusion.</td><td data-label=\"Question to examine together\">Which responses reveal reasoning, and what did you decide to do with that information?</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Lesson time and observed evidence\">14:10: During partner rehearsal, Chen asks student A why the quotation matters. A gives an oral explanation, then adds a related explanation to the revision.</td><td data-label=\"Connection and cautious interpretation\">The prompt and later work are consistent with improved expression in this instance. They do not prove that this prompt alone caused the change.</td><td data-label=\"Question to examine together\">What does the oral-to-written comparison tell us about the support A used?</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Lesson time and observed evidence\">22:40: Chen asks whether everyone has revised. Student B signals completion; the collected revision still repeats the evidence without explanation.</td><td data-label=\"Connection and cautious interpretation\">A completion check did not establish B's understanding of the reasoning goal. This is a possible missed assessment opportunity to discuss, not a judgment about B's effort.</td><td data-label=\"Question to examine together\">What brief check would distinguish a finished paragraph from an explanation that meets the goal?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are selected fictional notes, not a full observation record. In a real Part F, retain useful evidence across the actual lesson, including whole-class, small-group, and individual learning needs where observed. Record what the teacher did or did not adapt without inventing events to fill a rubric level.</p><p><strong>Keep the chronology clear:</strong> the completion question at lesson time 22:40 occurred late in this lesson. Reviewing that event and work B led to a plan for an earlier understanding check before independent revision in the next lesson. The proposed earlier check did not occur at 22:40.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-3\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Focused observation evidence</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate recognize, document, and analyze the volunteer teacher\u2019s teaching practice related to the selected CSTP element(s) and learning goals of the lesson?</p><p>Document useful, specific evidence linked to the selected elements and content goals.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides observational evidence that is unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals of the observed lesson. OR Candidate\u2019s observation notes are too vague to provide CSTP-related guidance. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Unrelated or vague notes, or absent analysis, do not support focused guidance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides evidence of the observed lesson that is mostly unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals. Candidate documents evidence that may be CSTP related, but evidence is vague and/or too general and may not be useful to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">General impressions provide limited information for improvement.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate accurately recognizes and documents observational evidence of essential teaching practices for the selected CSTP element(s) and content-specific student learning goals. Candidate\u2019s observation notes are detailed and clearly provide CSTP-specific guidance in a meaningful and useful way to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate clearly provides a clear analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect detailed, accurate notes and analysis to the selected elements and goal.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate\u2019s observational evidence documented either a. missed teaching opportunities that would have supported students to meet the content-specific learning goals OR b. successful CSTP-related practices that the volunteer teacher did implement that positively impacted student learning during the lesson.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Document a missed opportunity or successful practice and its learning connection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate documents how the volunteer teacher addresses whole-class, small-group, and individual student learning needs during the lesson and how they adapted or did not adapt their instruction to meet student needs as aligned to the learning goals of the lesson and the selected CSTP element(s).</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Examine whole-class, small-group, and individual needs and the teacher's response or nonresponse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 24 (printed page 20)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Timestamped events are separated from interpretations and linked to work. Three illustrative events are not a substitute for notes covering the actual observation.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-observation\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-observation\">Distinguish what you observed from what it means</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Invented lesson notes, 08:10-10:00: the teacher asks for evidence supporting a science claim. Seven learners volunteer; three give explanations that cite an observation. The other learners&#x27; reasoning is not recorded during this interval.</li><li>The pre-observation plan asks how the teacher checks understanding beyond volunteered answers. Independent exit responses will provide a separate source of evidence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>Everyone was engaged and understood the concept because students raised their hands.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>At 08:10-10:00, seven learners volunteered and three explanations cited an observation supporting the claim. These notes document responses from some learners, not the understanding of the whole class. In relation to our agreed assessment focus, the unanswered question is how the teacher checked the remaining learners&#x27; reasoning. I will keep that interpretation separate from the event record and compare it with the independent exit responses during our joint analysis.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Records an observable event and its scope before adding an interpretation.</li><li>Keeps participation and understanding distinct, and connects the limitation to the agreed observation focus.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>Can someone identify the event, the evidence, and your interpretation separately in your notes? Preserve real times and useful counterexamples. Your notes still need to address the actual CSTP element and lesson goals selected with the teacher.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Rubric 3.3</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do the notes separate observed events from interpretation and connect specific evidence to the agreed CSTP elements and learning goal?</li><li>Did you observe at least twenty minutes, record the full observation, and retain the agreed evidence in one notes file?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-g\">Continue to Part G <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"G\"><h2 id=\"part-g\">Part G: Student work used in the post-observation meeting</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | At least 1 example; 1 file; document or video; no maximum specified</p><p>Select actual work from the observed lesson that you and the teacher use in the post-observation conversation. Work can help distinguish an opportunity to learn from demonstrated understanding. Include the task or enough context to interpret the response, and protect student identities.</p><p>At least one example is required; a small set of contrasting examples may make the discussion more useful. Do not submit only the most successful work or replace an actual product with your description of what a student supposedly produced.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-G.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>Three fictional student-work excerpts</h3><p>Task: explain how returning a lost wallet supports a claim about responsibility. The source story and all student responses below are invented. The criterion is reasoning, not the length of the paragraph.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Work example</th><th scope=\"col\">Initial and revised work</th><th scope=\"col\">What the pair can and cannot show</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Work example\">A</td><td data-label=\"Initial and revised work\">Initial: \"The character is responsible. The character returns the wallet.\" Revised: \"Returning the wallet shows responsibility because the character protects another person's belongings even when no one is watching.\"</td><td data-label=\"What the pair can and cannot show\">The revision explains the relationship. Compare with the 14:10 interaction; do not assume every learner with the same language classification needs the same support.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Work example\">B</td><td data-label=\"Initial and revised work\">Initial: \"The character returns the wallet, so the character is responsible.\" Revised: \"The character is very responsible because the character returns the wallet.\"</td><td data-label=\"What the pair can and cannot show\">The revision remains close to restatement. More words and a because do not automatically demonstrate the intended reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Work example\">C</td><td data-label=\"Initial and revised work\">Initial: \"The character accepts responsibility by returning property that would have been easy to keep.\" Revised: \"Returning property that would have been easy to keep shows a choice to protect another person's interests.\"</td><td data-label=\"What the pair can and cannot show\">Reasoning is already visible in the initial response. Do not count all stronger final work as a new gain attributable to this lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The fictional class review finds the criterion in 9/28 initial paragraphs and 18/28 final paragraphs. These are descriptive within-lesson counts, not the separate Cycle 2 sample, a standardized growth estimate, or proof of lasting improvement. The post-meeting uses the contrasting work to understand the pattern.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Is at least one actual work example from the observed lesson included in one file and used in the post-meeting?</li><li>Are the task, criterion, identities, and initial/revised versions clear enough to support the claims made about learning?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-h\">Continue to Part H <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"H\"><h2 id=\"part-h\">Part H: Post-observation meeting clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | 1-3 clips; each at least 1 minute; up to 15 minutes TOTAL</p><p>Give the teacher the lesson recording before the post-observation meeting. Record the entire post-meeting, including joint viewing of lesson segments and examination of student work. Select meeting clips that make the evidence-based conversation, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching visible.</p><p>The classroom lesson recording is not itself the submitted Part H footage. The submitted evidence shows you and the teacher discussing that lesson. Keep both adults seen and heard and make shared references understandable within the selected material.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-H.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Recording</th><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative length</th><th scope=\"col\">Its role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Full observed lesson</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">28:00</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Record the whole observation, share with the teacher before the post-meeting, and jointly view selected segments during it. This is not a submitted meeting clip.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Post-meeting Clip 1</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">4:30</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Joint interpretation of learning using notes, lesson video, and work.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Post-meeting Clip 2</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">4:00</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Strengths, missed opportunity, and co-determined next steps with support.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Post-meeting Clip 3</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">3:30</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Teacher feedback on the candidate's coaching and its implications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The three submitted post-meeting clips total 12:00, and each is at least one minute. Part H permits 1-3 clips and up to 15 minutes combined; it does not permit 15 minutes per clip. Part I's ten-minute spoken alternative is separate commentary, not additional footage.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do authentic post-meeting clips show joint analysis of notes, lesson video and work, next steps, and teacher feedback, with both adults seen and heard?</li><li>Are there 1-3 clips, each at least one minute, totaling no more than fifteen minutes, and was the lesson recording shared before the meeting?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-i\">Continue to Part I <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"I\"><h2 id=\"part-i\">Part I: Commentary on the post-observation meeting</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Use the commentary to explain how your facilitation supported the teacher's interpretation of learning, joint analysis of teaching, next steps, and feedback about your coaching. Address every request within the three numbered prompts, not just the headings.</p><p>When a clip contains the viewing of a lesson segment, identify both references clearly: the submitted post-meeting clip timestamp locates your coaching; the lesson timestamp locates the classroom event under discussion. Do not blur them into a single unexplained time.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Download the original Part I template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-I-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-I-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>1. Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Assessment of Student Learning:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Describe how you supported the volunteer teacher in assessing student learning during the CSTP-focused lesson.</p><p>Describe how you shared the evidence of CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher.</p><p>Describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-analyzed the video of the lesson, student work, and/or other data (if collected) from the lesson.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address all three requests: the teacher's assessment of learning, how you shared CSTP-focused evidence, and how you co-analyzed the lesson video, work, and any other collected evidence.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative post-meeting Clip 1, 00:20-03:50: I first ask Chen what the revisions suggest. She identifies stronger explanation in A and continued repetition in B. We jointly view the lesson at 14:10, then compare the timestamped note with the two paragraphs. I ask what the teacher's prompt made available and what the work does not yet show. Chen concludes that oral rehearsal helped some students articulate a relationship but did not ensure an independent written explanation for everyone.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Separate lesson timestamps from submitted meeting-clip timestamps. The lesson video is evidence discussed in the meeting; Clip 1 is the recording of the adults analyzing it.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>2. Effectiveness of Teaching Practices Including Next Steps for Volunteer Teacher Growth:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Describe how you facilitated a joint discussion of the effectiveness of the volunteer teacher\u2019s teaching practices to support student learning, identifying any missed opportunities.</p><p>After discussing the volunteer teacher\u2019s effective teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-determined possible next steps of the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional growth in the identified CSTP element(s) (what you will do and what the volunteer teacher will do).</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain joint analysis of effective practice and missed opportunities, then how both adults determined next steps. Name what the teacher will do and what you will do.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative post-meeting Clip 2, 00:30-03:35: we connect the 08:20 model comparison to A's later explanation and the 22:40 closing check to B's unresolved repetition. Chen identifies a missed opportunity to check B's reasoning before independent revision. I ask what evidence would help her respond sooner. We agree she will add a brief explanation check and a contrasting example; I will co-plan the check, help review three work pairs, and meet with her in two weeks.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Do not turn coaching into a verdict delivered to the teacher. Identify strengths and growth through evidence and shared interpretation, with resources and additional support tied to the chosen CSTP focus.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>3. Volunteer Teacher Feedback on Candidate\u2019s Coaching Practices:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Describe the feedback the volunteer teacher provided regarding your coaching skills during the coaching and observation cycle, including what they found helpful and/or not helpful. Explain how this feedback informs your understanding of your strengths and areas for growth as an instructional coach.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Use the teacher's feedback on your coaching, including what was not helpful. This is not your feedback about the teacher's lesson.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative post-meeting Clip 3, 00:30-02:40: Chen says the side-by-side work and time to interpret the video were helpful, but my first summary came before she had finished explaining a concern. I paraphrase the concern and ask what would support her thinking. She requests that I ask for her interpretation before offering mine. This identifies a strength in evidence organization and a growth need in pacing and listening.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Praise alone rarely provides enough material for a useful analysis. Ask genuinely for feedback, then explain what it changes about your understanding of your coaching.</p></section><h3>A next-step plan that belongs to both adults</h3><p>Chen will design and use a brief explanation check before independent revision. The coach will co-plan two contrasting models and help review three initial/revised pairs, including a learner whose work remains unclear. They will meet in two weeks to ask whether the new check made student reasoning more visible and informed a useful instructional response. <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/PracticeGuide/22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">WWC, Teaching Secondary Students to Write Effectively</a> provides an evidence-based writing-instruction resource to examine together; it does not validate every assessment adaptation in this fictional plan.</p><p>The next lesson is where the earlier check will be tried. In Part I, cite the post-meeting clip time for the coaching exchange, then identify 22:40 separately as the lesson event the adults reviewed.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-4\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: A learning conversation</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to foster a learning conversation in a post-observation meeting using CSTP-focused observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student work with the volunteer teacher regarding strengths and area(s) for growth?</p><p>Use notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive partnership.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides inaccurate or irrelevant feedback to the volunteer teacher, making limited use of the observation and other collected evidence (e.g., lesson observation video, student work). OR No strengths or area(s) of growth for the volunteer teacher are discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Irrelevant feedback, missing strengths or growth, or no analysis leaves the conversation unsupported.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides the volunteer teacher with minimal CSTP-focused evidence or feedback, with limited discussion of findings. Candidate vaguely connects observation evidence, lesson observation video, or quality of student work to the volunteer teacher\u2019s strengths or area(s) for growth. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A vague evidence connection is not a rich learning conversation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate fosters a thoughtful and productive collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, citing the CSTP-specific observation evidence, the jointly viewed lesson observation video, and student work. Jointly with the volunteer teacher, candidate respectfully and supportively identifies teacher&#x27;s strengths and area(s) for growth based on gathered CSTP evidence and student work. In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Demonstrate joint evidence-based interpretation and analyze your facilitation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate makes deep connections with the volunteer teacher among the pre-observation meeting considerations, the CSTP observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student learning as evidenced in the student work produced during the lesson.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect the pre-meeting, observation, video, and student learning in depth.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate fosters a learning conversation that increases the volunteer teacher\u2019s capacity for self-evaluation of instruction and assessment of student learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Support the teacher's capacity to evaluate instruction and assess learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 25 (printed page 21)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Chen compares work and questions the effectiveness of a support. A teacher agreeing with the coach is not by itself evidence of greater self-evaluation capacity.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-5\"><h3>Rubric 3.5: Shared growth steps</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: In partnership with the volunteer teacher, how does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related evidence during the post-observation meeting?</p><p>Co-determine evidence-based next steps, resources, and coaching support.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate solely determines the volunteer teacher\u2019s next steps for professional growth. OR There is little to no identification of resources and/or additional coaching support for the volunteer teacher. OR No relationship to CSTP-related evidence for next steps is discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A candidate-assigned plan or missing support, evidence, or analysis is insufficient.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate occasionally allows the volunteer teacher to add their insights or perspective to the discussion on the teacher\u2019s next steps for professional growth. The conversation with the volunteer teacher lacks identification of resources and additional coaching support and/or the inclusion of CSTP-related evidence. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Occasional teacher input does not establish a developed joint plan.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate participates in a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher to co-determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support, that are clearly based on the CSTP-related evidence (observation feedback, lesson observation video, and/or student work). In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect shared next steps and support to the CSTP evidence; analyze the process.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate and the volunteer teacher identify professional growth with specific attention to addressing individual students\u2019 learning needs for CSTP-related professional learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Attend specifically to individual students' learning needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides specific evidenced-based resources to support the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional growth and engages the teacher in developing detailed next steps for professional learning and growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use specific evidence-based resources and detailed professional learning steps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 26 (printed page 22)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The plan gives both adults responsibilities and a follow-up date. It addresses work B without generalizing that need to an entire student group.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-analysis\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-analysis\">Let student work complicate a positive impression</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Fictional independent work review: of 24 collected responses, 6 state a correct claim with relevant evidence, 10 state a correct claim without supporting evidence, and 8 contain an inaccurate claim. These are distinct response categories, not longitudinal growth data.</li><li>In the invented post-observation conversation, the teacher compares this record with the volunteered lesson answers and proposes modeling how to connect an observation to a claim. The candidate agrees to help review the next independent responses with the teacher on Friday.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>Students participated well, so the lesson was successful. I told the teacher to use more modeling and the teacher agreed.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher and I compared the volunteered explanations with the independent work: only 6 of 24 collected responses connected a correct claim to relevant evidence. Ten offered a correct claim without support. That distinction challenged our favorable impression of the discussion. The teacher proposed modeling the connection between observation and claim, and I agreed to help review the next independent responses on Friday. This is a shared next step based on the work; we have not yet established improvement or the effect of the proposed modeling.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Examines the quality of student reasoning, not just a participation label or a total score.</li><li>Shows the teacher contributing the next step, the candidate&#x27;s support, and a follow-up point.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>Where do the lesson video and student work support or challenge each other? Cite the authentic post-observation exchange showing how both of you interpreted them and chose next steps. Do not infer classwide learning from selected successful work.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Rubric 3.4</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Rubric 3.5</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does every subprompt have a supported explanation, using the submitted meeting-clip timestamp separately from the lesson time being discussed?</li><li>Are jointly determined next steps, resources, and teacher feedback analyzed within eight pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-j\">Continue to Part J <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"J\"><h2 id=\"part-j\">Part J: Reflective narrative</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">4. Reflect | Up to 4 written pages OR 6 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Reflection turns the experience into professional learning. Examine the teacher's feedback and specific evidence from the cycle to explain your coaching strengths, limitations, and future changes. This is not a second evaluation of the teacher.</p><p>Two rubrics apply here. Rubric 3.6 concerns your coaching capacity and partnership; 3.7 concerns equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching within continuous improvement. Connect them without treating them as identical questions.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Download the original Part J template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-J-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>1. Based on your experience in this coaching cycle, and the feedback you elicited from the volunteer teacher, reflect on your instructional coaching abilities during the post-observation meeting in Step 3. Refer to the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback/input and evaluate your strengths and areas for growth in coaching and observing a teacher\u2019s practice and providing instructional feedback in relation to the agreed-upon CSTP element(s). Explain why you think you should work on them.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Evaluate your coaching strengths and growth needs using the teacher's feedback and the post-meeting evidence. Explain why the growth matters for the selected focus.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The comparison of work A and B helped us discuss assessment decisions instead of broad impressions of engagement. Chen's feedback that I summarized too soon shows a limitation: I can organize evidence while still narrowing the teacher's interpretation prematurely. Next time I will ask for her reading of the evidence, pause, and check my understanding before suggesting a conclusion. I will review the next recording with a mentor to examine whether the teacher's reasoning actually shapes our decision. <a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> helped me examine whether my summaries left room for teacher reflection. In the next conversation I will ask Chen to interpret a contrasting work pair before I summarize. This applies a professional-learning principle; it does not demonstrate an effect of one conversation.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The growth step responds to specific feedback. It is more informative than a promise to listen better without a way to examine that change.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>2. Reflect on and refer to evidence from Steps 1\u20133 to demonstrate how you were able to facilitate and maintain a two-way conversation throughout the coaching, observation, and instructional feedback cycle. How were you able to encourage teacher voice and ownership of their strengths and areas for growth based on evidence of practice in relation to the CSTP element(s), the video of instruction, and student work product(s)?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Trace the partnership through Steps 1-3 and show how teacher voice and ownership were maintained. Include evidence of choices and revisions, not just a claim that the conversation was two-way.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>In Part A, Chen's concern about delayed feedback shaped our schedule. In the pre-meeting, her self-assessment led to the assessment-domain focus and her request added instructional responses to the evidence plan. In the post-meeting, she interpreted the contrasting work before we agreed on the next check. Her criticism of my early summary shows that ownership was not perfect throughout; I need to maintain it during interpretation as carefully as I did during planning.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A balanced analysis can recognize both partnership and a moment that constrained it. Cite your actual records, clips, work, and feedback rather than inserting every artifact name into a paragraph.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>3. Informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focus on equitable leadership, reflect on your role (or future role) as an equity-driven school leader. Discuss the potential impact of supporting teacher growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reflect on your present or future role as an equity-driven instructional leader and the potential, not automatically proven, effects of coaching.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The lesson showed why completing a task is not the same as having an equitable opportunity to demonstrate reasoning. Coaching can help a teacher notice whose understanding is visible, which supports preserve the learning goal, and whose needs remain unmet. As a leader I would protect time for this kind of evidence-based inquiry and ensure access to useful coaching rather than reserve it for teachers already comfortable requesting help. One lesson and a supportive conversation do not establish lasting student improvement; follow-up work and teacher feedback are needed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Connect equitable leadership to concrete conditions for teacher and student learning. Research can inform that argument, but it cannot certify the effect of your single coaching cycle.</p></section><h3>Use research to examine your practice</h3><p><a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> describe professional learning that includes collaboration, modeling, coaching, feedback, reflection, and sustained opportunities to learn. Here that lens prompts a question about whether Chen had time and support to interpret evidence herself. It does not prove that this one coaching cycle improved student outcomes. Connect a source to a specific leadership choice and limitation, rather than attach a citation to a general claim that coaching works.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-6\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on coaching</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate analyze their capacity to conduct a CSTP-focused coaching and observation process, based on their experience in this coaching cycle and feedback from the volunteer teacher, and refer to evidence to demonstrate their ability to facilitate and maintain a coaching partnership?</p><p>Analyze strengths, growth, and evidence of maintaining a coaching partnership.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not provide an analysis of their capacity to support teacher development through CSTP-focused coaching and/or observation processes. OR Candidate does not use the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback regarding their coaching skills. OR Candidate does not reflect on or refer to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing analysis, teacher feedback, or partnership evidence leaves reflection incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate minimally describes strengths and/or areas for growth in CSTP-focused coaching and observation. Candidate\u2019s analysis provides cursory use of the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback. Candidate minimally reflects upon and/or vaguely refers to evidence of how they facilitated and/or maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Cursory feedback use and vague evidence do not explain your capacity.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate analyzes CSTP-focused coaching and observation strengths and clearly identifies areas for growth. Candidate\u2019s analysis clearly draws on the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback and other learning gained throughout the cycle. Candidate clearly reflects upon and refers to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect specific coaching strengths and growth to feedback and cycle evidence.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides an extensive analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback to them regarding their coaching and observation abilities in planning and conducting the coaching cycle and discusses how they would change their future approach to coaching based on that feedback.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze the feedback extensively and explain a changed future approach.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how evidence-based practices or research related to coaching skills support teacher development and/or adult learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use research or evidence-based practices to explain support for adult learning and teacher development.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 28 (printed page 24)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The reflection treats premature summarizing as a meaningful growth need, even though the teacher valued the evidence preparation.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-7\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, reflect on their role as an instructional leader acting as an instructional coach and reflect on the potential impact of coaching?</p><p>Connect equitable leadership, your coaching role, and potential teacher growth.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not demonstrate an awareness of the connection between equitable leadership and instructional coaching. OR Candidate does not reflect upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">No equity connection or no consideration of potential impact misses the purpose.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely reflects on and describes a minimal understanding of how instructional coaching and/or equitable leadership inform a continuous improvement mindset. Candidate minimally reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A general statement of fairness is not a developed leadership reflection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, clearly reflects on their role as a school leader acting as an instructional coach. Candidate clearly reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain your role, continuous improvement, and coaching's potential.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate cites specific evidence from the cycle, drawing on the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback and/or other collected evidence, to support their reflection and analysis of their capacity to be an equitable leader and an instructional coach.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Ground the reflection in specific cycle evidence or teacher feedback.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites evidence-based practices or research that support instructional coaching and observation as a viable and equitable strategy to support teacher growth and benefit students.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use research to support coaching as an equitable approach to teacher and student learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 29 (printed page 25)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The case distinguishes a potential pathway to better learning from a measured, sustained effect. An instructional example does not establish a score.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-feedback\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-feedback\">Learn from the support you did not yet provide</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Fictional teacher feedback values the observation notes but asks for more help deciding what to do with incomplete explanations. The conversation record shows that the candidate offered a resource before asking which aspect of the work was most puzzling.</li><li>The candidate plans to revisit the work with the teacher and use an open question before offering suggestions. No later coaching or learning results are in the record.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher appreciated my support, so my coaching was effective. I will keep providing resources to improve student outcomes.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher valued the observation notes but still needed help interpreting incomplete explanations. Reviewing our exchange, I noticed that I offered a resource before asking what the teacher wanted to understand. That may have narrowed the conversation too soon. In our next work review, I will invite the teacher&#x27;s interpretation first and ask which evidence supports it before offering options. I will seek feedback on whether that sequence helped the teacher make an instructional decision. I cannot yet report its effect on teaching or student learning.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Uses feedback to identify a specific coaching limitation rather than treating appreciation as proof of impact.</li><li>Connects a future change to teacher agency, evidence, and a way to evaluate the candidate&#x27;s own growth.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>What did the teacher find helpful, what was missing, and what in your record supports your interpretation? Explain how a specific coaching change could support equitable learning opportunities without promising outcomes your cycle has not demonstrated.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Rubric 3.6</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Rubric 3.7</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the reflection connect teacher feedback and cycle evidence to coaching strengths, growth, and equitable leadership?</li><li>Does the proposed change follow from that evidence, with claims about potential distinguished from demonstrated effects, within four pages OR six minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section></section><section id=\"final-check\"><h2>Review the cycle as one connected set of evidence</h2><p>Follow the selected CSTP elements through every part. Check that the pre-meeting plan identifies the evidence actually collected, the post-meeting uses that evidence with the teacher, and next steps arise from the joint analysis. The teacher's feedback should inform reflection rather than disappear after Part I.</p><p>Check the recording chain separately: full pre-meeting; at least a 20-minute fully recorded observation; lesson video shared in advance; full post-meeting including joint viewing; then correctly limited submitted meeting clips. Verify precise commentary timestamps against the final uploaded clips.</p><p>This page teaches the reasoning in expanded form. It is not a page-fit candidate submission or proof of completed permissions, observations, or coaching. Use your own evidence and responses, and inspect page counts and recording durations in the actual submission files.</p><p>Keep every required prompt and original formatting. Use Arial 11-point text, single spacing, and one-inch margins as directed. Protect identities and obtain required permissions. Check translation provisions, file formats, legibility, and every uploaded file. Upload every required file directly. A hyperlink to a document or video does not count as a submitted upload. Learning examples support understanding, not candidate response editing or clip selection.</p><p><a href=\"#readiness\">Submission preparation</a> &middot; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Complete official assessment guide</a> &middot; <a href=\"#connections/c3\">Preserved extended Harbor High example</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Original prompt text and all 35 rubric descriptors are reproduced from the supplied CTC Version 09 materials, copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Line wrapping is normalized. The official originals govern. The older Harbor High example remains a separate teaching case; do not merge its counts or events with Canyon View.</p></section></article>", "search": [{"title": "Cycle 3 Part A: School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-a", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"A\"><h2 id=\"part-a\">Part A: School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">1. Investigate | Up to 5 pages; 1 file</p><p>Start with the actual school and teacher, not a coaching script. Learn what observation and feedback currently mean in this setting, how teachers influence the process, and what the volunteer teacher has experienced. Those details affect trust and what support will be useful.</p><p>A volunteer partnership does not make you the teacher's evaluator. Clarify your role, the assessment purpose, permissions, and how evidence will be handled. The teacher's priorities and students' learning needs should shape the inquiry.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S1_A_CoachingObsFeedbackPracVolTeacher.docx\" download>Download the original Part A template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>1. Outline how often during the year teachers are coached, observed, or provided instructional feedback and what the intended outcomes are for the school, teachers, and students. After reviewing current practices at this school, provide an analysis of the effectiveness of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Separate coaching intended for growth from evaluative observations. Explain frequency, purpose, and the evidence you have for effectiveness; do not assume either process is effective because a calendar exists.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Canyon View schedules two administrator observations each year and offers voluntary coaching during a monthly inquiry period. The evaluation cycle checks performance expectations; the voluntary cycle is intended to help teachers examine a chosen instructional need. Teachers report that timely work-focused feedback is useful, but available records seldom connect follow-up to student work. The schedule establishes access to feedback, while its effect on teaching and learning remains incompletely documented.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Describe the practices at your actual site, even if they are limited or different. Analyze what the available evidence supports and what it cannot establish.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>2. Describe the processes, forms, and/or documents that are used to guide pre-observation meetings, collect observation data, and focus post-observation meetings (e.g., teacher response forms, observation tools, student work products, next steps for professional growth). If the school does not conduct teacher observations and uses a different approach to provide instructional feedback to teachers, provide a detailed description of the process(es) and forms used.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain the tools and processes actually used before, during, and after observation. If the site uses a different feedback process, describe it accurately.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The school uses a pre-conference form with a teacher goal, an observation note sheet with timestamps, and a post-conference form with strengths and next steps. The forms can support a connected inquiry, but recent examples emphasize teacher actions more than student responses. I will discuss with Chen how our notes can include both the instructional opportunity and evidence of student understanding.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A form's existence does not show how it is used. Explain its affordances and limitations for this particular coaching partnership.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>3. Explain the level of input teachers have on the focus or purpose of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe how much influence teachers have over the focus and purpose. Explain the implications for voice, trust, and ownership.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Teachers may request the voluntary coaching focus, while administrators set the evaluative observation schedule. Chen chose to examine assessment during writing instruction and asked that our work remain separate from evaluation. I clarified my non-evaluative role and the agreed handling of recordings before we set the focus.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Do not promise confidentiality beyond actual policy or your authority. Explain the real boundaries and how the teacher participates within them.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>4. Explain the implications for this cycle based on the analysis of the current role of coaching practices (at the school) and the background and experience of the volunteer teacher.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Use both the school context and this teacher's experience to explain how you will approach the cycle.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen has experience with instructional models but reports that earlier feedback arrived after the unit ended. We will schedule the post-observation conversation two days after the lesson and share the lesson recording beforehand. Because she wants to recognize student understanding during instruction, our pre-meeting will identify observable evidence rather than a broad goal such as engagement.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is the bridge from investigation to a tailored plan. The same generic coaching sequence for every teacher would not show how the investigation mattered.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Current Coaching and Observation Practices at the School</p><h3>5. How do the CSTPs guide or play a role in teacher growth and development at the school? (If the school uses another set of standards, provide a crosswalk to the CSTPs for the element[s] you have identified.)</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how the CSTP guide professional growth at the site. If another framework is used, provide a crosswalk for the selected elements instead of simply declaring the frameworks equivalent.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Canyon View uses the 2024 CSTP in voluntary growth plans. We will consider elements 5A, Understanding and Using Assessments, and 5B, Interpreting and Using Assessment Data to Inform Student Learning, in the same assessment domain. We will connect the selected focus to lesson evidence and Chen's self-assessment rather than treat the standards as a checklist of every possible teaching behavior.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The selected version and element wording must be consistent across the plan, observation, and commentary. Consult <a href=\"https://docs.ctc.ca.gov/Document/Download/30328\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">2024 California Standards for the Teaching Profession</a>. A crosswalk is needed when your site uses a different framework.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Volunteer Teacher and Context for the Coaching Cycle</p><h3>1. Provide information about the volunteer teacher\u2019s:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. professional experience as a teacher (including years of service, school site[s], grades and content taught, languages)</p><p>b. current assignment (grade level[s], content area, course titles)</p><p>c. past experiences with coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p><p>d. knowledge and use of the CSTP to guide their growth and development as a teacher at the school site, in other previous teaching positions, or during teacher preparation</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address all four subparts with relevant, appropriately obtained background: experience and languages, assignment, prior feedback, and CSTP knowledge.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen has taught for seven years at two secondary schools, previously Grades 7-8 English and now Grade 9 English. She speaks English and Mandarin and teaches three Grade 9 sections. She has participated in annual administrator observations and a voluntary peer lesson study. She found concrete work comparisons helpful but found generalized ratings difficult to act on. She has used CSTP elements in a growth plan but wants help making the assessment focus observable.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Language and cultural knowledge are assets, not automatic explanations of teaching effectiveness. Include the background that changes your coaching choices and protect identities.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Volunteer Teacher and Context for the Coaching Cycle</p><h3>2. Explain how the information you gathered about the school context and the volunteer teacher\u2019s experiences informs your plans for conducting a coaching cycle, including how you will:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. orient the volunteer teacher to participate in your coaching process</p><p>b. participate in a pre-observation meeting</p><p>c. prepare for the observation and collection of evidence (including student work)</p><p>d. jointly conduct (with the volunteer teacher) the post-observation meeting</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain each stage of the plan, not merely the dates. Show how the teacher will influence decisions and how student work enters the process.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Orientation will establish purpose, consent, roles, storage, and the teacher's right to ask questions. In the pre-meeting, Chen will assess her practice and review the lesson and students before we jointly select one or two elements. We will agree on timestamped notes and initial/revised paragraphs as evidence. I will record the full 28-minute observation, share the video with her before the post-meeting, and co-conduct a discussion using her interpretation, jointly viewed segments, and student work.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The four stages form one inquiry. Teacher ownership is visible in choices, not just in the fact that the teacher attended.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q8\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Volunteer Teacher and Context for the Coaching Cycle</p><h3>3. Describe any challenges you may face in conducting a coaching cycle with the volunteer teacher and provide strategies for addressing these challenges.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Anticipate actual partnership, scheduling, access, or evidence challenges and practical responses.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>A short planning period may encourage rushed conclusions, so we will reserve uninterrupted time and identify the evidence to review in advance. Chen worries the camera could make the lesson feel evaluative; we will clarify the non-evaluative purpose and permissions without promising anonymity that the recording cannot provide. We will test sound and framing and use authorized storage. If required consent is unavailable, I will work with the program and site on an appropriate alternative before recording.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A challenge should lead to a feasible response within policy. Do not solve a permission problem by recording anyway or fabricating evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-1\"><h3>Rubric 3.1: Coaching context</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school with the volunteer teacher, and explain the implications of how current practices impact their approach to conducting a coaching cycle?</p><p>Analyze current practices and tailor the coaching cycle to this teacher.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not provide an explanation of the purpose or processes for coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school. Candidate outlines a plan for the coaching cycle with little or no details OR does not outline a plan.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing purpose or a plan leaves the coaching approach unexplained.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate lists the teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback practices at the school, providing limited details about the processes used. The purpose for conducting these processes is unclear. Candidate outlines a vague plan for the coaching cycle with minimal details about implications of each step of the coaching cycle and/or observation based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A list of practices and a vague plan do not show meaningful implications.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly describes and analyzes the current role of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback, discussing the purpose and intended goals of the practices for both teachers and students. Candidate clearly describes a plan for and the implications of each step of the coaching cycle and observation based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional experience, current teaching assignment, and past experience in relation to coaching.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze the setting and explain each stage using the teacher's experience.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes how teachers participate in the selection and purpose of the instructional feedback practices at the school to meet the unique needs of the teachers and how the practices build trust between the teachers and school administrators.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain teacher influence and how feedback practices build trust.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate discusses relevant evidence-based practices and/or adult learning theory to explain how school practices of teacher coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback support ongoing teacher development and benefit students\u2019 learning and well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use relevant evidence-based practice or adult-learning theory to explain benefits.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Coaching, Observation, and/or Instructional Feedback Practices at the School and the Volunteer Teacher (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 14 (printed page 10)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The plan responds to Chen's experience with delayed feedback and her request for a non-evaluative focus.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-context\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-context\">Separate coaching conditions from coaching impact</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Fictional school records show annual evaluation conferences, while voluntary coaching started this term. Schoolwide scores declined before the new coaching began; no evaluation of that coaching is available.</li><li>The volunteer teacher says prior feedback felt evaluative and requests help interpreting student explanations. The candidate is not the teacher&#x27;s evaluator.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>Declining school scores prove coaching is ineffective. I will use a standard coaching cycle with this teacher.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The schoolwide trend raises a concern but cannot establish the effect of a coaching process that began later. The teacher&#x27;s prior experience gives me a more immediate planning consideration: feedback has felt evaluative. I will clarify my non-evaluative role, invite the teacher to identify a focus, and agree on what evidence we will examine. The request to interpret student explanations should shape that conversation; it does not authorize me to select the teacher&#x27;s goal in advance.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Respects timing and the limits of schoolwide outcome data.</li><li>Uses the teacher&#x27;s experience to shape the partnership instead of merely describing their background.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>Which feature of this teacher&#x27;s experience changes your plan? Explain the implications without treating whole-school achievement trends as a direct measure of one coaching practice.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\">Rubric 3.1</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Have you analyzed school coaching practices and the volunteer teacher's experience, self-assessment, and learning needs?</li><li>Does the coaching plan respond to this teacher and explain your relationship, including relevant authority and trust?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-b\">Continue to Part B <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part B: Classroom context, lesson, and observation", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-b", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"B\"><h2 id=\"part-b\">Part B: Classroom context, lesson, and observation</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Up to 4 pages; 1 file</p><p>This part helps the reader understand the classroom in which the lesson and coaching focus make sense. The goal is not to list every fact you know about the class. Explain the content, students, setting, supports, and practical conditions that matter for interpreting instruction.</p><p>Gather this information with the teacher. A statement such as diverse learners is too broad to guide observation; identify relevant assets, interests, language resources, prior learning, and support needs without disclosing protected information.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_B_ClassroomContextLessonObservation.docx\" download>Download the original Part B template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>1. Classroom context, including:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. topic of lesson, learning and instructional goals, students\u2019 prior knowledge, and what was taught in the prior lesson</p><p>b. classroom setting (face-to-face, online, or hybrid) and organization for learning (whole class, small groups, individual practice, hybrid)</p><p>c. description of students served, including their assets and learning needs (e.g., grade level, demographics, student assets [cultural and/or linguistic] and interests, learning supports and adaptations)</p><p>d. materials and/or resources used during the lesson by the teacher or students to enhance or extend learning</p><p>e. other relevant classroom context information (e.g., time of day for the lesson, time of year, support staff that are in the room during the lesson, materials used, safety issues, bell schedule, parent support)</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address every context subpart. Describe the content goal, prior learning, setting and grouping, student assets and needs, materials, and relevant logistics. Include details that will affect what the observer can reasonably interpret.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The 28-minute face-to-face Grade 9 English lesson asks students to explain how textual evidence supports an argument. In the prior lesson, students selected quotations; nine of this class's 28 preliminary paragraphs explained their relevance. Students bring experience discussing fairness in stories and varied languages and community perspectives. Twelve are classified as English learners; supports are chosen from individual needs, not assigned uniformly by label. Three students have documented accommodations, which remain in place for this lesson. Students compare models as a class, rehearse explanations with a partner, and revise individually. Materials include the text excerpt, contrasting models, an explanation criterion, accessible directions, and optional language supports. Morgan co-teaches this midmorning lesson; a shortened bell schedule is not in effect.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This class of 28 is not the 24-student cross-class sample in the Cycle 2 case. Do not merge their denominators. In an actual response, explain specific relevant assets, adaptations, and logistics without exposing protected student information.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the four-page account establish the actual class, content goal, prior learning, learner assets, and needed access supports?</li><li>Are the jointly chosen CSTP elements, evidence plan, and lesson context consistent with the pre-meeting?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-c\">Continue to Part C <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part C: Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-c", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"C\"><h2 id=\"part-c\">Part C: Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Use the volunteer teacher's lesson plan, including a description of the student work product. It should make the learning goal, planned instruction, and evidence of learning understandable. The candidate is not asked to replace the teacher's planning with a generic coach-authored lesson.</p><p>Review how students will show the intended learning and how the work will be available for the post-observation conversation. A plan describes intention; the lesson recording, observation notes, and actual work later show what occurred.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-C.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part C. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>Fictional lesson-plan excerpt</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Time</th><th scope=\"col\">Planned teaching and learning</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence and access</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">0-5 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Recall the claim and selected quotation; review the explanation criterion.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Initial short paragraph; accessible text and directions.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">5-10 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Compare a reasoned explanation with a repetition of evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Students identify why one explanation supports the claim.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">10-18 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Partner rehearsal, then an individual explanation.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Teacher checks responses; optional language supports and documented accommodations remain available.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">18-25 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Revise the paragraph after a check for reasoning.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Keep initial and revised versions; teacher responds to observed needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Time\">25-28 minutes</td><td data-label=\"Planned teaching and learning\">Explain which revision strengthened the argument and why.</td><td data-label=\"Evidence and access\">Brief exit response and revised paragraph reveal understanding and remaining questions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The work-product description identifies the initial paragraph, revision, and explanation of the revision. The observation will examine how Chen gathers evidence and responds, not whether every planned minute is followed exactly. This excerpt is a fictional teaching aid, not an original CTC lesson-plan template.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Is this the volunteer teacher's lesson plan with a clear learning goal and description of the work students will produce?</li><li>Can the teacher and coach identify what evidence will be available for the later conversation?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-d\">Continue to Part D <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part D: Two pre-observation meeting clips", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-d", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"D\"><h2 id=\"part-d\">Part D: Two pre-observation meeting clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Exactly 2 video files; up to 6 minutes each</p><p>Record the pre-observation meeting with the candidate and teacher seen and heard, following required permissions. Select two clips that together show the required planning work. These are clips of the adult meeting, not excerpts from the classroom lesson.</p><p>The conversation should include teacher self-assessment, review of the lesson and students, shared selection of one or two elements from the same CSTP domain, and agreement on the observation and evidence. Keep the teacher's reasoning visible, including how it influences decisions.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-D.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part D. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative meeting clip</th><th scope=\"col\">Length</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Illustrative meeting clip\">Pre-meeting Clip 1</td><td data-label=\"Length\">5:10</td><td data-label=\"Evidence coverage\">Teacher self-assessment; lesson plan, work product, context, and learner assets and needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Illustrative meeting clip\">Pre-meeting Clip 2</td><td data-label=\"Length\">5:00</td><td data-label=\"Evidence coverage\">Joint element selection; agreed observation process and specific evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Both clips fit the individual six-minute maximum. A combined duration below twelve minutes does not allow one seven-minute clip. These are invented selection illustrations; no staged or simulated interaction should be submitted as actual fieldwork.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are there exactly two authentic pre-observation meeting clips, each at most six minutes, with both adults seen and heard?</li><li>Do they show teacher self-assessment, review of students and lesson, shared CSTP selection, and agreement on evidence?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-e\">Continue to Part E <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part E: Commentary on the pre-observation meeting", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-e", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"E\"><h2 id=\"part-e\">Part E: Commentary on the pre-observation meeting</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Address each prompt at least once across the two submitted clips. Identify the clip and a precise timestamp, then explain what you did, why, and the impact supported by the conversation. The commentary template's repeated boxes give you room for additional moments; they are not a request to invent additional requirements.</p><p>Analysis is more than a summary. \u201cI asked about prior learning\u201d describes an action. Explaining why that question was needed and how the teacher's response changed the evidence plan makes the leadership reasoning visible.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S2_E_CommentaryPreObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Download the original Part E template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-E-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-E-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part E template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>1. Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Self-Assessment: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher determined their instructional needs and challenges based on the CSTP element(s).</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how the teacher's self-assessment informed the shared understanding of instructional needs. Describe your listening and questioning, not only the need you selected.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 1, 00:35-01:45: Chen says students can identify evidence but she is not always sure who understands its connection to a claim. I ask what she notices in their work and which responses are hardest to interpret. She contrasts a correct quotation with a paragraph that explains why it matters. This makes the assessment need more specific before we choose the observation focus.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The analysis links a facilitative question to a teacher response. Your actual clip must show the evidence for the impact you claim.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>2. Lesson Plan Review: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher reviewed the lesson plan. Think about:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. student work product,</p><p>b. classroom context, and</p><p>c. students\u2019 assets and learning needs.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Connect review of the lesson plan to the work students will produce, the classroom setting, and learner assets and needs.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 1, 02:00-04:20: we review the initial/revised paragraph task and Chen describes students who explain ideas more fully in discussion than in writing. I ask how the task will make their thinking visible without lowering the reasoning goal. She proposes oral rehearsal and accessible models, followed by an individual written explanation. We agree to retain both versions of selected work.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Do not treat student needs as a list of labels. Explain how the discussion changes the task, support, or evidence plan.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>3. CSTP Selection: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher selected one or two CSTP elements based on the volunteer teacher\u2019s self-assessment and students\u2019 assets and learning needs.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show a joint selection of one or two elements in the same domain, grounded in self-assessment and students, not imposed by the candidate.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 2, 00:20-01:50: Chen identifies assessment as the focus. We compare 5A and 5B with her question about recognizing reasoning and responding while the lesson is underway. She proposes collecting both brief checks for understanding and the instructional responses that follow; together we retain these two elements in the assessment domain.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The teacher's contribution must influence the choice. The fact that you read element titles aloud does not establish joint selection.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part E</p><h3>4. Coaching and Observation Process: Describe how you and the volunteer teacher collaboratively reviewed the coaching and observation process, and the CSTP\u2013element-focused data to be collected during the lesson.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how you agreed on the observation process and specific evidence, including student work.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative pre-meeting Clip 2, 02:10-04:35: I propose timestamped notes separating what is seen or heard from interpretation. Chen asks that we record which student responses prompt a change in instruction and retain initial/revised paragraphs from contrasting learners. We agree to record the full 28-minute lesson, share the recording with Chen in advance, and hold a post-observation meeting in which we view the video and analyze student work together.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A data plan should allow both adults to recognize what will be collected and why. A general promise to observe engagement would not answer the agreed assessment question.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-2\"><h3>Rubric 3.2: Planning a coaching partnership</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher to understand the learning goals, classroom context, and student assets (e.g., cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests and learning needs; jointly select with the volunteer teacher one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, including evidence to be collected; and plan for the observation?</p><p>Analyze a two-way pre-meeting grounded in students, lesson goals, and selected CSTP elements.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate and the volunteer teacher do not discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs. Selection of the CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected, including student work, is made by the candidate without input from the volunteer teacher or element(s) are not selected. OR No plan is established for the coaching and/or observation process. OR In the commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">One-sided selection, missing planning, or absent analysis can leave essential evidence missing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate and volunteer teacher broadly discuss classroom context, learning goals, and/or student assets and/or learning needs, including student work. Candidate minimally involves the volunteer teacher in the selection of CSTP element(s) and/or evidence to be collected during the observation. It is not clear what aspects of instruction need to be the focus of the observation, and a plan is not specified and/or agreed to by the volunteer teacher OR candidate creates plan for observation without input from the volunteer teacher. In the commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis* of their ability to listen to and/or talk with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Minimal teacher involvement is not a clearly shared plan.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly engages the volunteer teacher in a detailed and focused pre-observation discussion about the classroom context, student assets and learning needs, and content-specific learning goals of the lesson. Candidate and the volunteer teacher jointly select the CSTP element(s) and together determine evidence to be collected during the observation, including student work. Candidate and volunteer teacher create a plan for the observation, clearly identifying the focal aspects of instruction. In the commentary, candidate provides an analysis* of their ability to listen to and talk with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Demonstrate focused discussion, joint selection, a data plan, and analysis of your communication.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies to engage in a two-way discussion with the volunteer teacher to draw out their specific areas of need in relation to learning goals, classroom context, student assets, CSTP element(s), and observation plans.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use facilitative questions to draw out the teacher's specific needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses facilitative questioning strategies, including re-directing as appropriate for the conversation, to deepen discussion regarding equitable opportunities for all students in the class to learn.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Deepen discussion of equitable learning opportunities, redirecting when appropriate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Note: *Analysis: What the candidate does, why they do it, and the impact their action had Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Classroom Context, Lesson, and Observation (up to 4 pages) \u2022 Part C: Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Lesson Plan, Including Student Work Product Description \u2022 Part D: 2 Video Clips of the Pre-Observation Meeting (up to 6 minutes each) \u2022 Part E: Commentary of the Pre-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 19 (printed page 15)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The fictional moments show how teacher self-assessment changes the focus and evidence plan; real submitted clips must demonstrate the candidate's own actions.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-focus\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-focus\">Connect a shared focus to observable evidence</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Invented pre-observation record: the teacher wants to distinguish students stating a science claim from explaining it with evidence. The teacher and candidate review the applicable CSTP wording and select an assessment-focused element together.</li><li>They agree to collect question-and-response notes during discussion and each learner&#x27;s independent claim-and-evidence exit response. The teacher requests that observations distinguish volunteered answers from the whole class&#x27;s understanding.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>We selected a CSTP and I will observe engagement. The teacher agreed with my plan.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher&#x27;s concern was that a few volunteered answers might overstate class understanding. We jointly connected that concern to the assessment-focused CSTP element in our plan. We agreed on two evidence sources: notes on the questions and responses during discussion, and independent claim-and-evidence exit responses. The teacher&#x27;s request changed the collection plan: I would not treat volunteered answers alone as evidence of everyone&#x27;s understanding.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Shows teacher agency through a request that actually shapes the plan.</li><li>Connects the focus to evidence that can answer the agreed question; does not equate agreement with a co-designed plan.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>In your full response, identify the exact agreed CSTP element and applicable wording, the teacher&#x27;s self-assessment, and the evidence plan. Check your current guide and standards; do not transfer an older sample&#x27;s element number without verifying it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\">Rubric 3.2</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are all four prompts addressed across the two clips with precise timestamps and analysis of your facilitation?</li><li>Does commentary agree with the actual planning conversation and fit eight pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-f\">Continue to Part F <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part F: Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-f", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"F\"><h2 id=\"part-f\">Part F: Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Observe the lesson live (synchronously, while it is taught) for at least 20 minutes and record the full observation. This case uses a 28-minute lesson. Collect the specific evidence agreed on in the pre-meeting, including student work. Keep notes descriptive enough that the teacher can examine the event rather than accept your judgment on trust.</p><p>Separate observation from interpretation: what was said or done, when it happened, which selected element it informs, and what question it raises. Avoid unsupported labels such as disengaged or ineffective. A note can document a missed opportunity without pretending to know the teacher's intention.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-F.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part F. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>From an event to a useful coaching question</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Lesson time and observed evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Connection and cautious interpretation</th><th scope=\"col\">Question to examine together</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Lesson time and observed evidence\">08:20: Chen displays two explanations and asks which better supports the claim. Four students refer to the because clause; two responses only identify the quotation.</td><td data-label=\"Connection and cautious interpretation\">5A/5B: the comparison elicits evidence of understanding. Responses suggest different interpretations of the criterion, not a single whole-class conclusion.</td><td data-label=\"Question to examine together\">Which responses reveal reasoning, and what did you decide to do with that information?</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Lesson time and observed evidence\">14:10: During partner rehearsal, Chen asks student A why the quotation matters. A gives an oral explanation, then adds a related explanation to the revision.</td><td data-label=\"Connection and cautious interpretation\">The prompt and later work are consistent with improved expression in this instance. They do not prove that this prompt alone caused the change.</td><td data-label=\"Question to examine together\">What does the oral-to-written comparison tell us about the support A used?</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Lesson time and observed evidence\">22:40: Chen asks whether everyone has revised. Student B signals completion; the collected revision still repeats the evidence without explanation.</td><td data-label=\"Connection and cautious interpretation\">A completion check did not establish B's understanding of the reasoning goal. This is a possible missed assessment opportunity to discuss, not a judgment about B's effort.</td><td data-label=\"Question to examine together\">What brief check would distinguish a finished paragraph from an explanation that meets the goal?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are selected fictional notes, not a full observation record. In a real Part F, retain useful evidence across the actual lesson, including whole-class, small-group, and individual learning needs where observed. Record what the teacher did or did not adapt without inventing events to fill a rubric level.</p><p><strong>Keep the chronology clear:</strong> the completion question at lesson time 22:40 occurred late in this lesson. Reviewing that event and work B led to a plan for an earlier understanding check before independent revision in the next lesson. The proposed earlier check did not occur at 22:40.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-3\"><h3>Rubric 3.3: Focused observation evidence</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate recognize, document, and analyze the volunteer teacher\u2019s teaching practice related to the selected CSTP element(s) and learning goals of the lesson?</p><p>Document useful, specific evidence linked to the selected elements and content goals.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides observational evidence that is unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals of the observed lesson. OR Candidate\u2019s observation notes are too vague to provide CSTP-related guidance. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Unrelated or vague notes, or absent analysis, do not support focused guidance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides evidence of the observed lesson that is mostly unrelated to the CSTP element(s) and/or student learning goals. Candidate documents evidence that may be CSTP related, but evidence is vague and/or too general and may not be useful to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">General impressions provide limited information for improvement.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate accurately recognizes and documents observational evidence of essential teaching practices for the selected CSTP element(s) and content-specific student learning goals. Candidate\u2019s observation notes are detailed and clearly provide CSTP-specific guidance in a meaningful and useful way to the volunteer teacher. In their commentary, candidate clearly provides a clear analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s qualities of teaching practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect detailed, accurate notes and analysis to the selected elements and goal.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate\u2019s observational evidence documented either a. missed teaching opportunities that would have supported students to meet the content-specific learning goals OR b. successful CSTP-related practices that the volunteer teacher did implement that positively impacted student learning during the lesson.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Document a missed opportunity or successful practice and its learning connection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate documents how the volunteer teacher addresses whole-class, small-group, and individual student learning needs during the lesson and how they adapted or did not adapt their instruction to meet student needs as aligned to the learning goals of the lesson and the selected CSTP element(s).</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Examine whole-class, small-group, and individual needs and the teacher's response or nonresponse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 24 (printed page 20)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Timestamped events are separated from interpretations and linked to work. Three illustrative events are not a substitute for notes covering the actual observation.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-observation\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-observation\">Distinguish what you observed from what it means</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Invented lesson notes, 08:10-10:00: the teacher asks for evidence supporting a science claim. Seven learners volunteer; three give explanations that cite an observation. The other learners&#x27; reasoning is not recorded during this interval.</li><li>The pre-observation plan asks how the teacher checks understanding beyond volunteered answers. Independent exit responses will provide a separate source of evidence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>Everyone was engaged and understood the concept because students raised their hands.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>At 08:10-10:00, seven learners volunteered and three explanations cited an observation supporting the claim. These notes document responses from some learners, not the understanding of the whole class. In relation to our agreed assessment focus, the unanswered question is how the teacher checked the remaining learners&#x27; reasoning. I will keep that interpretation separate from the event record and compare it with the independent exit responses during our joint analysis.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Records an observable event and its scope before adding an interpretation.</li><li>Keeps participation and understanding distinct, and connects the limitation to the agreed observation focus.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>Can someone identify the event, the evidence, and your interpretation separately in your notes? Preserve real times and useful counterexamples. Your notes still need to address the actual CSTP element and lesson goals selected with the teacher.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\">Rubric 3.3</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do the notes separate observed events from interpretation and connect specific evidence to the agreed CSTP elements and learning goal?</li><li>Did you observe at least twenty minutes, record the full observation, and retain the agreed evidence in one notes file?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-g\">Continue to Part G <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part G: Student work used in the post-observation meeting", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-g", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"G\"><h2 id=\"part-g\">Part G: Student work used in the post-observation meeting</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | At least 1 example; 1 file; document or video; no maximum specified</p><p>Select actual work from the observed lesson that you and the teacher use in the post-observation conversation. Work can help distinguish an opportunity to learn from demonstrated understanding. Include the task or enough context to interpret the response, and protect student identities.</p><p>At least one example is required; a small set of contrasting examples may make the discussion more useful. Do not submit only the most successful work or replace an actual product with your description of what a student supposedly produced.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-G.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>Three fictional student-work excerpts</h3><p>Task: explain how returning a lost wallet supports a claim about responsibility. The source story and all student responses below are invented. The criterion is reasoning, not the length of the paragraph.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Work example</th><th scope=\"col\">Initial and revised work</th><th scope=\"col\">What the pair can and cannot show</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Work example\">A</td><td data-label=\"Initial and revised work\">Initial: \"The character is responsible. The character returns the wallet.\" Revised: \"Returning the wallet shows responsibility because the character protects another person's belongings even when no one is watching.\"</td><td data-label=\"What the pair can and cannot show\">The revision explains the relationship. Compare with the 14:10 interaction; do not assume every learner with the same language classification needs the same support.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Work example\">B</td><td data-label=\"Initial and revised work\">Initial: \"The character returns the wallet, so the character is responsible.\" Revised: \"The character is very responsible because the character returns the wallet.\"</td><td data-label=\"What the pair can and cannot show\">The revision remains close to restatement. More words and a because do not automatically demonstrate the intended reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Work example\">C</td><td data-label=\"Initial and revised work\">Initial: \"The character accepts responsibility by returning property that would have been easy to keep.\" Revised: \"Returning property that would have been easy to keep shows a choice to protect another person's interests.\"</td><td data-label=\"What the pair can and cannot show\">Reasoning is already visible in the initial response. Do not count all stronger final work as a new gain attributable to this lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The fictional class review finds the criterion in 9/28 initial paragraphs and 18/28 final paragraphs. These are descriptive within-lesson counts, not the separate Cycle 2 sample, a standardized growth estimate, or proof of lasting improvement. The post-meeting uses the contrasting work to understand the pattern.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Is at least one actual work example from the observed lesson included in one file and used in the post-meeting?</li><li>Are the task, criterion, identities, and initial/revised versions clear enough to support the claims made about learning?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-h\">Continue to Part H <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part H: Post-observation meeting clips", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-h", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"H\"><h2 id=\"part-h\">Part H: Post-observation meeting clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | 1-3 clips; each at least 1 minute; up to 15 minutes TOTAL</p><p>Give the teacher the lesson recording before the post-observation meeting. Record the entire post-meeting, including joint viewing of lesson segments and examination of student work. Select meeting clips that make the evidence-based conversation, shared next steps, and teacher feedback on your coaching visible.</p><p>The classroom lesson recording is not itself the submitted Part H footage. The submitted evidence shows you and the teacher discussing that lesson. Keep both adults seen and heard and make shared references understandable within the selected material.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/guide-part-H.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Recording</th><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative length</th><th scope=\"col\">Its role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Full observed lesson</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">28:00</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Record the whole observation, share with the teacher before the post-meeting, and jointly view selected segments during it. This is not a submitted meeting clip.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Post-meeting Clip 1</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">4:30</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Joint interpretation of learning using notes, lesson video, and work.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Post-meeting Clip 2</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">4:00</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Strengths, missed opportunity, and co-determined next steps with support.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Recording\">Post-meeting Clip 3</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative length\">3:30</td><td data-label=\"Its role\">Teacher feedback on the candidate's coaching and its implications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The three submitted post-meeting clips total 12:00, and each is at least one minute. Part H permits 1-3 clips and up to 15 minutes combined; it does not permit 15 minutes per clip. Part I's ten-minute spoken alternative is separate commentary, not additional footage.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do authentic post-meeting clips show joint analysis of notes, lesson video and work, next steps, and teacher feedback, with both adults seen and heard?</li><li>Are there 1-3 clips, each at least one minute, totaling no more than fifteen minutes, and was the lesson recording shared before the meeting?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-i\">Continue to Part I <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part I: Commentary on the post-observation meeting", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-i", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"I\"><h2 id=\"part-i\">Part I: Commentary on the post-observation meeting</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Use the commentary to explain how your facilitation supported the teacher's interpretation of learning, joint analysis of teaching, next steps, and feedback about your coaching. Address every request within the three numbered prompts, not just the headings.</p><p>When a clip contains the viewing of a lesson segment, identify both references clearly: the submitted post-meeting clip timestamp locates your coaching; the lesson timestamp locates the classroom event under discussion. Do not blur them into a single unexplained time.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S3_I_CommentaryPostObservationMeeting.docx\" download>Download the original Part I template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-I-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-I-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>1. Volunteer Teacher\u2019s Assessment of Student Learning:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Describe how you supported the volunteer teacher in assessing student learning during the CSTP-focused lesson.</p><p>Describe how you shared the evidence of CSTP-focused data with the volunteer teacher.</p><p>Describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-analyzed the video of the lesson, student work, and/or other data (if collected) from the lesson.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address all three requests: the teacher's assessment of learning, how you shared CSTP-focused evidence, and how you co-analyzed the lesson video, work, and any other collected evidence.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative post-meeting Clip 1, 00:20-03:50: I first ask Chen what the revisions suggest. She identifies stronger explanation in A and continued repetition in B. We jointly view the lesson at 14:10, then compare the timestamped note with the two paragraphs. I ask what the teacher's prompt made available and what the work does not yet show. Chen concludes that oral rehearsal helped some students articulate a relationship but did not ensure an independent written explanation for everyone.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Separate lesson timestamps from submitted meeting-clip timestamps. The lesson video is evidence discussed in the meeting; Clip 1 is the recording of the adults analyzing it.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>2. Effectiveness of Teaching Practices Including Next Steps for Volunteer Teacher Growth:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Describe how you facilitated a joint discussion of the effectiveness of the volunteer teacher\u2019s teaching practices to support student learning, identifying any missed opportunities.</p><p>After discussing the volunteer teacher\u2019s effective teaching practices that supported student learning in this lesson, describe how you and the volunteer teacher co-determined possible next steps of the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional growth in the identified CSTP element(s) (what you will do and what the volunteer teacher will do).</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain joint analysis of effective practice and missed opportunities, then how both adults determined next steps. Name what the teacher will do and what you will do.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative post-meeting Clip 2, 00:30-03:35: we connect the 08:20 model comparison to A's later explanation and the 22:40 closing check to B's unresolved repetition. Chen identifies a missed opportunity to check B's reasoning before independent revision. I ask what evidence would help her respond sooner. We agree she will add a brief explanation check and a contrasting example; I will co-plan the check, help review three work pairs, and meet with her in two weeks.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Do not turn coaching into a verdict delivered to the teacher. Identify strengths and growth through evidence and shared interpretation, with resources and additional support tied to the chosen CSTP focus.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>3. Volunteer Teacher Feedback on Candidate\u2019s Coaching Practices:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Describe the feedback the volunteer teacher provided regarding your coaching skills during the coaching and observation cycle, including what they found helpful and/or not helpful. Explain how this feedback informs your understanding of your strengths and areas for growth as an instructional coach.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Use the teacher's feedback on your coaching, including what was not helpful. This is not your feedback about the teacher's lesson.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative post-meeting Clip 3, 00:30-02:40: Chen says the side-by-side work and time to interpret the video were helpful, but my first summary came before she had finished explaining a concern. I paraphrase the concern and ask what would support her thinking. She requests that I ask for her interpretation before offering mine. This identifies a strength in evidence organization and a growth need in pacing and listening.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Praise alone rarely provides enough material for a useful analysis. Ask genuinely for feedback, then explain what it changes about your understanding of your coaching.</p></section><h3>A next-step plan that belongs to both adults</h3><p>Chen will design and use a brief explanation check before independent revision. The coach will co-plan two contrasting models and help review three initial/revised pairs, including a learner whose work remains unclear. They will meet in two weeks to ask whether the new check made student reasoning more visible and informed a useful instructional response. <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/PracticeGuide/22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">WWC, Teaching Secondary Students to Write Effectively</a> provides an evidence-based writing-instruction resource to examine together; it does not validate every assessment adaptation in this fictional plan.</p><p>The next lesson is where the earlier check will be tried. In Part I, cite the post-meeting clip time for the coaching exchange, then identify 22:40 separately as the lesson event the adults reviewed.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-4\"><h3>Rubric 3.4: A learning conversation</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to foster a learning conversation in a post-observation meeting using CSTP-focused observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student work with the volunteer teacher regarding strengths and area(s) for growth?</p><p>Use notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive partnership.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides inaccurate or irrelevant feedback to the volunteer teacher, making limited use of the observation and other collected evidence (e.g., lesson observation video, student work). OR No strengths or area(s) of growth for the volunteer teacher are discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Irrelevant feedback, missing strengths or growth, or no analysis leaves the conversation unsupported.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides the volunteer teacher with minimal CSTP-focused evidence or feedback, with limited discussion of findings. Candidate vaguely connects observation evidence, lesson observation video, or quality of student work to the volunteer teacher\u2019s strengths or area(s) for growth. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A vague evidence connection is not a rich learning conversation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate fosters a thoughtful and productive collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher, citing the CSTP-specific observation evidence, the jointly viewed lesson observation video, and student work. Jointly with the volunteer teacher, candidate respectfully and supportively identifies teacher&#x27;s strengths and area(s) for growth based on gathered CSTP evidence and student work. In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to foster a learning conversation.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Demonstrate joint evidence-based interpretation and analyze your facilitation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate makes deep connections with the volunteer teacher among the pre-observation meeting considerations, the CSTP observation evidence, lesson observation video, and student learning as evidenced in the student work produced during the lesson.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect the pre-meeting, observation, video, and student learning in depth.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate fosters a learning conversation that increases the volunteer teacher\u2019s capacity for self-evaluation of instruction and assessment of student learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Support the teacher's capacity to evaluate instruction and assess learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 25 (printed page 21)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Chen compares work and questions the effectiveness of a support. A teacher agreeing with the coach is not by itself evidence of greater self-evaluation capacity.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-5\"><h3>Rubric 3.5: Shared growth steps</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: In partnership with the volunteer teacher, how does the candidate demonstrate and analyze their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support based on the CSTP-related evidence during the post-observation meeting?</p><p>Co-determine evidence-based next steps, resources, and coaching support.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate solely determines the volunteer teacher\u2019s next steps for professional growth. OR There is little to no identification of resources and/or additional coaching support for the volunteer teacher. OR No relationship to CSTP-related evidence for next steps is discussed. OR In their commentary, candidate does not provide an analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A candidate-assigned plan or missing support, evidence, or analysis is insufficient.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate occasionally allows the volunteer teacher to add their insights or perspective to the discussion on the teacher\u2019s next steps for professional growth. The conversation with the volunteer teacher lacks identification of resources and additional coaching support and/or the inclusion of CSTP-related evidence. In their commentary, candidate provides a limited analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Occasional teacher input does not establish a developed joint plan.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate participates in a collaborative conversation with the volunteer teacher to co-determine next steps for professional growth, including resources and additional coaching support, that are clearly based on the CSTP-related evidence (observation feedback, lesson observation video, and/or student work). In their commentary, candidate provides a clear analysis of their ability to co-determine next steps for professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect shared next steps and support to the CSTP evidence; analyze the process.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate and the volunteer teacher identify professional growth with specific attention to addressing individual students\u2019 learning needs for CSTP-related professional learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Attend specifically to individual students' learning needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides specific evidenced-based resources to support the volunteer teacher\u2019s professional growth and engages the teacher in developing detailed next steps for professional learning and growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use specific evidence-based resources and detailed professional learning steps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part F: Specific Notes from the Observation and/or Forms Used to Document the Observation Evidence Related to the CSTP Element(s) \u2022 Part G: Student Work Product Example(s) from the Lesson Used in the Post-Observation Meeting \u2022 Part H: 1 to 3 Video Clips of the Post-Observation Meeting (up to 15 minutes of video total; a single clip should be at least 1 minute in length) \u2022 Part I: Commentary of the Post-Observation Meeting (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 26 (printed page 22)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The plan gives both adults responsibilities and a follow-up date. It addresses work B without generalizing that need to an entire student group.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-analysis\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-analysis\">Let student work complicate a positive impression</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Fictional independent work review: of 24 collected responses, 6 state a correct claim with relevant evidence, 10 state a correct claim without supporting evidence, and 8 contain an inaccurate claim. These are distinct response categories, not longitudinal growth data.</li><li>In the invented post-observation conversation, the teacher compares this record with the volunteered lesson answers and proposes modeling how to connect an observation to a claim. The candidate agrees to help review the next independent responses with the teacher on Friday.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>Students participated well, so the lesson was successful. I told the teacher to use more modeling and the teacher agreed.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher and I compared the volunteered explanations with the independent work: only 6 of 24 collected responses connected a correct claim to relevant evidence. Ten offered a correct claim without support. That distinction challenged our favorable impression of the discussion. The teacher proposed modeling the connection between observation and claim, and I agreed to help review the next independent responses on Friday. This is a shared next step based on the work; we have not yet established improvement or the effect of the proposed modeling.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Examines the quality of student reasoning, not just a participation label or a total score.</li><li>Shows the teacher contributing the next step, the candidate&#x27;s support, and a follow-up point.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>Where do the lesson video and student work support or challenge each other? Cite the authentic post-observation exchange showing how both of you interpreted them and chose next steps. Do not infer classwide learning from selected successful work.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\">Rubric 3.4</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\">Rubric 3.5</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does every subprompt have a supported explanation, using the submitted meeting-clip timestamp separately from the lesson time being discussed?</li><li>Are jointly determined next steps, resources, and teacher feedback analyzed within eight pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/part-j\">Continue to Part J <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 Part J: Reflective narrative", "url": "#cycle/c3/part-j", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"J\"><h2 id=\"part-j\">Part J: Reflective narrative</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">4. Reflect | Up to 4 written pages OR 6 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Reflection turns the experience into professional learning. Examine the teacher's feedback and specific evidence from the cycle to explain your coaching strengths, limitations, and future changes. This is not a second evaluation of the teacher.</p><p>Two rubrics apply here. Rubric 3.6 concerns your coaching capacity and partnership; 3.7 concerns equitable instructional leadership and the potential of coaching within continuous improvement. Connect them without treating them as identical questions.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Download the original Part J template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle3/templates/part-J-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 3 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>1. Based on your experience in this coaching cycle, and the feedback you elicited from the volunteer teacher, reflect on your instructional coaching abilities during the post-observation meeting in Step 3. Refer to the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback/input and evaluate your strengths and areas for growth in coaching and observing a teacher\u2019s practice and providing instructional feedback in relation to the agreed-upon CSTP element(s). Explain why you think you should work on them.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Evaluate your coaching strengths and growth needs using the teacher's feedback and the post-meeting evidence. Explain why the growth matters for the selected focus.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The comparison of work A and B helped us discuss assessment decisions instead of broad impressions of engagement. Chen's feedback that I summarized too soon shows a limitation: I can organize evidence while still narrowing the teacher's interpretation prematurely. Next time I will ask for her reading of the evidence, pause, and check my understanding before suggesting a conclusion. I will review the next recording with a mentor to examine whether the teacher's reasoning actually shapes our decision. <a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> helped me examine whether my summaries left room for teacher reflection. In the next conversation I will ask Chen to interpret a contrasting work pair before I summarize. This applies a professional-learning principle; it does not demonstrate an effect of one conversation.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The growth step responds to specific feedback. It is more informative than a promise to listen better without a way to examine that change.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>2. Reflect on and refer to evidence from Steps 1\u20133 to demonstrate how you were able to facilitate and maintain a two-way conversation throughout the coaching, observation, and instructional feedback cycle. How were you able to encourage teacher voice and ownership of their strengths and areas for growth based on evidence of practice in relation to the CSTP element(s), the video of instruction, and student work product(s)?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Trace the partnership through Steps 1-3 and show how teacher voice and ownership were maintained. Include evidence of choices and revisions, not just a claim that the conversation was two-way.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>In Part A, Chen's concern about delayed feedback shaped our schedule. In the pre-meeting, her self-assessment led to the assessment-domain focus and her request added instructional responses to the evidence plan. In the post-meeting, she interpreted the contrasting work before we agreed on the next check. Her criticism of my early summary shows that ownership was not perfect throughout; I need to maintain it during interpretation as carefully as I did during planning.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A balanced analysis can recognize both partnership and a moment that constrained it. Cite your actual records, clips, work, and feedback rather than inserting every artifact name into a paragraph.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>3. Informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focus on equitable leadership, reflect on your role (or future role) as an equity-driven school leader. Discuss the potential impact of supporting teacher growth through coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reflect on your present or future role as an equity-driven instructional leader and the potential, not automatically proven, effects of coaching.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The lesson showed why completing a task is not the same as having an equitable opportunity to demonstrate reasoning. Coaching can help a teacher notice whose understanding is visible, which supports preserve the learning goal, and whose needs remain unmet. As a leader I would protect time for this kind of evidence-based inquiry and ensure access to useful coaching rather than reserve it for teachers already comfortable requesting help. One lesson and a supportive conversation do not establish lasting student improvement; follow-up work and teacher feedback are needed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Connect equitable leadership to concrete conditions for teacher and student learning. Research can inform that argument, but it cannot certify the effect of your single coaching cycle.</p></section><h3>Use research to examine your practice</h3><p><a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> describe professional learning that includes collaboration, modeling, coaching, feedback, reflection, and sustained opportunities to learn. Here that lens prompts a question about whether Chen had time and support to interpret evidence herself. It does not prove that this one coaching cycle improved student outcomes. Connect a source to a specific leadership choice and limitation, rather than attach a citation to a general claim that coaching works.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-6\"><h3>Rubric 3.6: Reflection on coaching</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate analyze their capacity to conduct a CSTP-focused coaching and observation process, based on their experience in this coaching cycle and feedback from the volunteer teacher, and refer to evidence to demonstrate their ability to facilitate and maintain a coaching partnership?</p><p>Analyze strengths, growth, and evidence of maintaining a coaching partnership.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not provide an analysis of their capacity to support teacher development through CSTP-focused coaching and/or observation processes. OR Candidate does not use the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback regarding their coaching skills. OR Candidate does not reflect on or refer to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing analysis, teacher feedback, or partnership evidence leaves reflection incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate minimally describes strengths and/or areas for growth in CSTP-focused coaching and observation. Candidate\u2019s analysis provides cursory use of the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback. Candidate minimally reflects upon and/or vaguely refers to evidence of how they facilitated and/or maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Cursory feedback use and vague evidence do not explain your capacity.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate analyzes CSTP-focused coaching and observation strengths and clearly identifies areas for growth. Candidate\u2019s analysis clearly draws on the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback and other learning gained throughout the cycle. Candidate clearly reflects upon and refers to evidence of how they facilitated and maintained a coaching partnership with the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Connect specific coaching strengths and growth to feedback and cycle evidence.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides an extensive analysis of the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback to them regarding their coaching and observation abilities in planning and conducting the coaching cycle and discusses how they would change their future approach to coaching based on that feedback.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze the feedback extensively and explain a changed future approach.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how evidence-based practices or research related to coaching skills support teacher development and/or adult learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use research or evidence-based practices to explain support for adult learning and teacher development.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 28 (printed page 24)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The reflection treats premature summarizing as a meaningful growth need, even though the teacher valued the evidence preparation.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-3-7\"><h3>Rubric 3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, reflect on their role as an instructional leader acting as an instructional coach and reflect on the potential impact of coaching?</p><p>Connect equitable leadership, your coaching role, and potential teacher growth.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not demonstrate an awareness of the connection between equitable leadership and instructional coaching. OR Candidate does not reflect upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">No equity connection or no consideration of potential impact misses the purpose.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely reflects on and describes a minimal understanding of how instructional coaching and/or equitable leadership inform a continuous improvement mindset. Candidate minimally reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A general statement of fairness is not a developed leadership reflection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate, informed by a continuous improvement mindset and focused on equitable leadership, clearly reflects on their role as a school leader acting as an instructional coach. Candidate clearly reflects upon the potential impact of coaching, observation, and/or instructional feedback to support teacher growth.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain your role, continuous improvement, and coaching's potential.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate cites specific evidence from the cycle, drawing on the volunteer teacher\u2019s feedback and/or other collected evidence, to support their reflection and analysis of their capacity to be an equitable leader and an instructional coach.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Ground the reflection in specific cycle evidence or teacher feedback.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites evidence-based practices or research that support instructional coaching and observation as a viable and equitable strategy to support teacher growth and benefit students.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use research to support coaching as an equitable approach to teacher and student learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 29 (printed page 25)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The case distinguishes a potential pathway to better learning from a measured, sustained effect. An instructional example does not establish a score.</p></section><section class=\"writing-practice\"><h3>Strengthen your explanation</h3><p class=\"source-note\">Original fictional practice, not candidate evidence or a scored answer. Use only the supplied record when comparing these paragraphs. <a href=\"#cycle/c3/stronger-writing\">About these comparisons</a>.</p><details class=\"writing-example\" data-writing-example=\"c3-feedback\"><summary id=\"writing-c3-feedback\">Learn from the support you did not yet provide</summary><div class=\"writing-evidence\"><h4>Fictional evidence record</h4><ul><li>Fictional teacher feedback values the observation notes but asks for more help deciding what to do with incomplete explanations. The conversation record shows that the candidate offered a resource before asking which aspect of the work was most puzzling.</li><li>The candidate plans to revisit the work with the teacher and use an open question before offering suggestions. No later coaching or learning results are in the record.</li></ul></div><div class=\"writing-developing\"><h4>Developing explanation</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher appreciated my support, so my coaching was effective. I will keep providing resources to improve student outcomes.</p></blockquote></div><div class=\"writing-stronger\"><h4>Stronger explanation of the same record</h4><blockquote><p>The teacher valued the observation notes but still needed help interpreting incomplete explanations. Reviewing our exchange, I noticed that I offered a resource before asking what the teacher wanted to understand. That may have narrowed the conversation too soon. In our next work review, I will invite the teacher&#x27;s interpretation first and ask which evidence supports it before offering options. I will seek feedback on whether that sequence helped the teacher make an instructional decision. I cannot yet report its effect on teaching or student learning.</p></blockquote></div><h4>What makes the reasoning stronger</h4><ul><li>Uses feedback to identify a specific coaching limitation rather than treating appreciation as proof of impact.</li><li>Connects a future change to teacher agency, evidence, and a way to evaluate the candidate&#x27;s own growth.</li></ul><h4>Apply this to your own work</h4><p>What did the teacher find helpful, what was missing, and what in your record supports your interpretation? Explain how a specific coaching change could support equitable learning opportunities without promising outcomes your cycle has not demonstrated.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Related Version 09 focus: <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\">Rubric 3.6</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\">Rubric 3.7</a>. This excerpt addresses only the named reasoning move, not every requirement in those rubrics.</p></details></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the reflection connect teacher feedback and cycle evidence to coaching strengths, growth, and equitable leadership?</li><li>Does the proposed change follow from that evidence, with claims about potential distinguished from demonstrated effects, within four pages OR six minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 3 limits and rubric map", "url": "#cycle/c3/limits", "html": "<section id=\"limits\"><h2>What you submit, and the limits to plan around</h2><p>Read this before arranging meetings or collecting evidence. These are maxima, not targets. The original narrative template counts toward its page limit. N/A in the guide means no page or time maximum is specified for that part; it does not remove the evidence requirement.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Limit and files</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">School coaching practices and the volunteer teacher</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Classroom context, lesson, and observation</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Volunteer teacher lesson plan and work-product description</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Two pre-observation meeting clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Exactly 2 video files; up to 6 minutes each</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">E</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Commentary on the pre-observation meeting</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">F</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and CSTP-focused evidence</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">G</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work used in the post-observation meeting</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">At least 1 example; 1 file; document or video; no maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">H</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Post-observation meeting clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1-3 clips; each at least 1 minute; up to 15 minutes TOTAL</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">I</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Commentary on the post-observation meeting</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 written pages OR 6 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Observe the lesson live (synchronously, while it is taught) for at least 20 minutes and record the full observation. Share the lesson recording with the teacher before the post-observation meeting, and jointly view selected lesson evidence during that meeting. The lesson recording itself is not the submitted pre- or post-meeting footage.</p><p>Part D is exactly two clips of up to six minutes each. Part H is 1-3 clips with at least one minute per clip and at most 15 minutes total. Parts E and I each allow up to eight written pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL; Part J allows four pages OR six minutes. Do not interchange these limits.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C3_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Original submission specifications</a></p></section><section id=\"foundation\"><h2>Understand the work before filling in the templates</h2><h3>Begin with partnership</h3><p>The teacher brings knowledge of students, content, prior instruction, and professional learning needs. You contribute a process that helps both of you examine practice. Facilitative questions invite the teacher to interpret evidence and consider possibilities; they are not leading questions whose only acceptable answer is your preferred solution.</p><h3>Choose a focus narrow enough to observe</h3><p>Jointly select one or two CSTP elements from the same domain, using the teacher's self-assessment, lesson, and students' assets and needs. Agree on what evidence would be relevant. A broad interest in engagement needs to become an observable question before it can guide data collection. Use the applicable CSTP version and an explicit crosswalk when the site uses another framework.</p><h3>Keep the evidence roles distinct</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it establishes</th><th scope=\"col\">What it does not establish alone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Lesson plan</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Intended learning, instruction, supports, and work product.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">What actually happened.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Observation notes and lesson video</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">Events and interactions during the observed lesson.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">The meaning of every student's experience or lasting learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Student work</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">What particular learners demonstrated on the task.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">Why the result occurred, without further analysis.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Meeting clips and commentary</td><td data-label=\"What it establishes\">The coaching interaction and your analysis of it.</td><td data-label=\"What it does not establish alone\">An event not present in the submitted evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section id=\"rubric-map\"><h2>How the parts and rubrics fit together</h2><p>Each row names one rubric. Read its purpose now, then return to the complete five-level rubric beside the relevant work. A higher level includes the stated earlier levels; extra pages, polished language, or more charts alone do not establish it.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-overview-map\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Rubric focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">1. Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Understand the school and the volunteer teacher.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-1\"><strong>3.1: Coaching context</strong></a><br>Analyze current practices and tailor the coaching cycle to this teacher.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">2. Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B-E</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Listen, select the focus together, and agree on evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-2\"><strong>3.2: Planning a coaching partnership</strong></a><br>Analyze a two-way pre-meeting grounded in students, lesson goals, and selected CSTP elements.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">F-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Recognize and analyze teaching in relation to learning.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-3\"><strong>3.3: Focused observation evidence</strong></a><br>Document useful, specific evidence linked to the selected elements and content goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">F-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Co-analyze evidence, strengths, and growth.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-4\"><strong>3.4: A learning conversation</strong></a><br>Use notes, jointly viewed lesson video, and student work in a productive partnership.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">F-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Turn findings into supported professional learning.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-5\"><strong>3.5: Shared growth steps</strong></a><br>Co-determine evidence-based next steps, resources, and coaching support.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">4. Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Use teacher feedback to examine your coaching.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-6\"><strong>3.6: Reflection on coaching</strong></a><br>Analyze strengths, growth, and evidence of maintaining a coaching partnership.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">4. Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Consider the potential of coaching within continuous improvement.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c3/rubric-3-7\"><strong>3.7: Equitable instructional leadership</strong></a><br>Connect equitable leadership, your coaching role, and potential teacher growth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section id=\"fictional-case\"><h2>Fictional Example Case</h2><p class=\"source-note\">All school context, people, meetings, work samples, quotations, counts, and outcomes in this Canyon View case are invented for instruction. This is not Jordan High data, an assessed submission, or evidence that fieldwork occurred. Illustrative video moments are not recordings or scripts to stage.</p><p>The volunteer teacher is Chen, one of the fictional Canyon View colleagues introduced in Cycle 2. Her participation here is a separate, voluntary coaching partnership, not an automatic continuation imposed by the earlier group. She chooses to examine how assessment during a Grade 9 writing lesson informs her response to students.</p><p>They jointly select two assessment-domain elements in the 2024 CSTP, 5A and 5B. A 28-minute lesson produces initial and revised paragraphs. The post-meeting compares timestamped observation notes, jointly viewed lesson segments, and three contrasting work examples. Chen also gives feedback about the candidate's coaching. The inquiry concerns this class of 28; it is distinct from Cycle 2's 24-student sample.</p><p>The case shows useful reasoning and limitations, not a model guaranteed to earn a score. A real teacher may choose a different focus, and that choice should follow the evidence and partnership rather than a desire to copy the example.</p></section>"}]};
